window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-22"]=[{"id":"q3-89","q":"3:89","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:89 · Repentance, spiritual lineage and the return to companionship","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 378–378","cairo":"I:378","source_page_first":378,"source_page_last":378,"ar":"إِلاَّ الَّذِينَ تابُوا مِنْ بَعْدِ ذلِكَ وَأَصْلَحُوا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ (89)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إلا مَن تابَ من بعد الردة، فأسلم وأصلح ما أفسد، فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ له فيما فعل، رَحِيمٌ به حيث تاب.\nولمّا نزلت الآية حملها إليه رجلٌ من قومه وقرأها عليه، فقال الحارث: إنك والله فيما علمت لصدوق، وإن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم لأصدق منك، وإن الله- تعالى- لأصدق الثلاثة، فرجع الحارث إلى المدينة، فأسلم وحَسُن إسلامُه.\nالإشارة: كل مَن ابتغى الخصوصية من غير أهلها، أو ادعاها ولم يأخذْها من معدنها، فلن تُقبل منه، وهو عند القوم من الخاسرين في طريق الخصوص، فكل مَن لا شيخ له في هذا الشأن فهو لقيط، لا أب له، دعي، لا نسب له.\nوالمراد بأهلها: العارفون بالله، أهلُ الفناء والبقاء، أهل الجذب والسلوك، أهل السكر والصحو، الذين شربوا الخمر فسكروا ثم صحوا وتكملوا، فمعدنُ الخصوصية عند هؤلاء، فكل من لم يصحبهم ولم يشرب من خمرتهم، لا يُقتدى به، ولو بلغ من الكرامة ما بلغ، وأخسر من هذا مِنْ صحب أهل هذه الخمرة، وشهد بأن طريقهم حق، ثم رجع عنها، فهذا مغبون ملعون عند كافة الخلق، أي: مطرود عن شهود الحق، إلا مَن تاب ورجع إلى صحبتهم والأدب معهم، فإن الله غفور رحيم.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى من ارتدّ وبقي على كفره، حتى مات، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِلاَّ الَّذِينَ تابُوا مِنْ بَعْدِ ذلِكَ وَأَصْلَحُوا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ (89)","quran_en":"89. Except those who repent after that and set things right: God is forgiving, merciful.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Repentance joined to repair","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: except one who repents after renunciation, submits to God and repairs what he has corrupted. God forgives what he has done and has mercy upon him when he repents.<\/p><p>When the verse was revealed, a man from al-Ḥārith’s people carried it to him and recited it. Al-Ḥārith said: “By God, I have known you to speak truth. The Prophet is more truthful than you, and God Most High is the most truthful of the three.” Al-Ḥārith returned to Medina, embraced Islam and became a good Muslim.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: except one who repents after renunciation, submits to God and repairs what he has corrupted. God forgives what he has done and has mercy upon him when he repents. When the verse was revealed, a man from al-Ḥārith’s people carried it to him and recited it. Al-Ḥārith said: “By God, I have known you to speak truth. The Prophet is more truthful than you, and God Most High is the most truthful of the three.” Al-Ḥārith returned to Medina, embraced Islam and became a good Muslim."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The source of spiritual distinction and the possibility of returning","html":"<p>Whoever seeks special spiritual distinction (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخصوصية<\/bdi>) from those who do not possess it, or claims it without receiving it from its source, will not have it accepted from him. Among the Folk he is one of the losers in the special path. Whoever has no shaykh in this matter is a foundling without a father, a pretender without a lineage. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N048\">[XIV-N048]<\/a><\/p><p>Those who possess it are the knowers of God: the people of effacement and subsistence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفناء والبقاء<\/bdi>), attraction and wayfaring (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجذب والسلوك<\/bdi>), intoxication and sobriety (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السكر والصحو<\/bdi>), who drank the wine and became intoxicated, then returned to sobriety and attained completion. With them is the source of special spiritual distinction. Whoever has not accompanied them or drunk of their wine is not to be taken as a guide, however far his extraordinary gifts (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الكرامات<\/bdi>) extend.<\/p><p>Still more bereft is one who accompanied the people of this wine, bore witness that their path is true, and then turned back from it. He has suffered loss and is accursed among all creatures—that is, driven away from witnessing the Real—except one who repents and returns to their companionship and to right conduct with them. God is forgiving, merciful.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever seeks special spiritual distinction ( الخصوصية ) from those who do not possess it, or claims it without receiving it from its source, will not have it accepted from him. Among the Folk he is one of the losers in the special path. Whoever has no shaykh in this matter is a foundling without a father, a pretender without a lineage. [XIV-N048] Those who possess it are the knowers of God: the people of effacement and subsistence ( الفناء والبقاء ), attraction and wayfaring ( الجذب والسلوك ), intoxication and sobriety ( السكر والصحو ), who drank the wine and became intoxicated, then returned to sobriety and attained completion. With them is the source of special spiritual distinction. Whoever has not accompanied them or drunk of their wine is not to be taken as a guide, however far his extraordinary gifts ( الكرامات ) extend. Still more bereft is one who accompanied the people of this wine, bore witness that their path is true, and then turned back from it. He has suffered loss and is accursed among all creatures—that is, driven away from witnessing the Real—except one who repents and returns to their companionship and to right conduct with them. God is forgiving, merciful."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High then mentioned one who renounced belief and remained in unbelief until death, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High then mentioned one who renounced belief and remained in unbelief until death, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1815,"arabic_characters":1115},{"id":"q3-90","q":"3:90","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:90 · Persistence in rejection, repentance and the danger of spiritual relapse","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 378–379","cairo":"I:378–379","source_page_first":378,"source_page_last":379,"ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا بَعْدَ إِيمانِهِمْ ثُمَّ ازْدادُوا كُفْراً لَنْ تُقْبَلَ تَوْبَتُهُمْ وَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الضَّالُّونَ (90)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ الَّذِينَ ارتدوا عن الإيمان، ثُمَّ ازْدادُوا في الكفر، وقالوا: نتربص بمحمد ريب المنون، لَنْ تُقْبَلَ تَوْبَتُهُمْ أي: لا توبة لهم فتقبل، لأنه سبق لهم الشقاء، أو لأنهم لا يتوبون إلا عند الغرغرة، أو لَنْ تُقْبَلَ تَوْبَتُهُمْ ما داموا على كفرهم. وَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الضَّالُّونَ المنهمكون في الضلالة.\nقيل: نزلت في أصحاب الحارث بن سويد المتقدم، وكانوا أحد عشر رجلاً، لما رجع الحارثُ قالوا: نقيم بمكة على الكفر ما بَدا لنا، فمتى أردنا الرجعة رجعنا، فلما افتتح النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم مكة، دخل في الإسلام بعضُهم، فقُبلت توبته، وبقي من بقي على كفره، فنزلت الآية فيهم. وقيل: نزلت في اليهود، كفروا بعيسى بعد إيمانهم بأنبيائهم، ثم ازدادوا كفراً بكفرهم بمحمد صلى الله عليه وسلم، وقيل: نزلت في النصارى كفروا بمحمد صلى الله عليه وسلم بعد إيمانهم بعيسى، ثُمَّ ازْدادُوا كُفْراً بإصرارهم عليه. وقيل: نزلت في الفريقين معا، كفروا بنبينا محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم بعد إيمانهم به قبل ظهوره، ثُمَّ ازْدادُوا كُفْراً بتمردهم فيه، وتماديهم على المعاصي. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن من دَخَل طريق التربية، وأخذ في تهذيب نفسه وتطهيرها من المساوئ وأوساخ الحس، ثم غلبته القهرية ورجع عنها، فإن تاب قريباً ورجع إليها سهل عليه الرجوع، ورجى نجحه وقبلت توبته، وإن استمر على رجوعه عنها حتى ألفت نفسه البطالة لن ترجى توبته وصار من الضالين، فمثله كآنية، فرَّغت منها لبناً أو عسلاً، وعَمَرْتها بالقطران، فإن بادرت بإهراقه منها قريباً سهل غسلها، وإن أمهلتها حتى صبغ فيها عسر غسلها، وتعذر زوال رائحته منها. [فإن مات على رجعته فلا يحشر في الآخرة مع أهل هذه الرفقة، ولو شفع فيه ألف عارف، بل من كمال المكر به أن يُلقي شَبَهَه في الآخرة على غيره، حتى يتوهم عارفوه من أهل المعرفة أنه هو، فلا يخطر بباله أنه يشفع فيه] . قاله القشيري.\nقال المحشي: وما ذكره ربما ينظر إلى قضية الخليل مع أبيه، حين يلقاه وعليه القترة، فيريد الشفاعة له، فيمسخ ذيخاً(1) متلطخاً- أي: خنزيراً- فينكره، كما في الحديث الصحيح، فتذكر واعتبر. هـ. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم ذكر من مات على كفره، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا بَعْدَ إِيمانِهِمْ ثُمَّ ازْدادُوا كُفْراً لَنْ تُقْبَلَ تَوْبَتُهُمْ وَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الضَّالُّونَ (90)","quran_en":"90. Those who disbelieve after having believed, and then increase in unbelief—their repentance will not be accepted. Those are the ones who have gone astray.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Several explanations of repentance that is not accepted","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: those who turn back from faith and then increase in unbelief, saying, “We shall wait for the turns of fortune to overtake Muḥammad”—“their repentance will not be accepted.” That is, they will have no repentance that could be accepted, because wretchedness has already been decreed for them; or because they repent only when the death-rattle comes; or their repentance will not be accepted for as long as they remain in their unbelief. “Those are the ones who have gone astray”: those who have plunged deeply into error.<\/p><p>It is said that this was revealed concerning the companions of al-Ḥārith ibn Suwayd mentioned above. They were eleven men. When al-Ḥārith returned, they said: “We shall remain in Mecca in unbelief for as long as it suits us; whenever we wish to return, we shall return.” When the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, conquered Mecca, some of them entered Islam and their repentance was accepted, while the others remained in unbelief. The verse was revealed concerning those who remained.<\/p><p>It is also said that it was revealed concerning the Jews: they disbelieved in Jesus after believing in their prophets, then increased in unbelief by disbelieving in Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him. Or concerning the Christians: they disbelieved in Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, after believing in Jesus, then increased in unbelief by persisting in it. Or concerning both groups together: they disbelieved in our Prophet Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, after believing in him before his appearance, then increased in unbelief by obstinately rejecting him and persisting in acts of disobedience. And God Most High knows best. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N049\">[XIV-N049]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: those who turn back from faith and then increase in unbelief, saying, “We shall wait for the turns of fortune to overtake Muḥammad”—“their repentance will not be accepted.” That is, they will have no repentance that could be accepted, because wretchedness has already been decreed for them; or because they repent only when the death-rattle comes; or their repentance will not be accepted for as long as they remain in their unbelief. “Those are the ones who have gone astray”: those who have plunged deeply into error. It is said that this was revealed concerning the companions of al-Ḥārith ibn Suwayd mentioned above. They were eleven men. When al-Ḥārith returned, they said: “We shall remain in Mecca in unbelief for as long as it suits us; whenever we wish to return, we shall return.” When the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, conquered Mecca, some of them entered Islam and their repentance was accepted, while the others remained in unbelief. The verse was revealed concerning those who remained. It is also said that it was revealed concerning the Jews: they disbelieved in Jesus after believing in their prophets, then increased in unbelief by disbelieving in Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him. Or concerning the Christians: they disbelieved in Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, after believing in Jesus, then increased in unbelief by persisting in it. Or concerning both groups together: they disbelieved in our Prophet Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, after believing in him before his appearance, then increased in unbelief by obstinately rejecting him and persisting in acts of disobedience. And God Most High knows best. [XIV-N049]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Returning before an acquired habit becomes entrenched","html":"<p>Know that someone may enter the path of spiritual training (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التربية<\/bdi>) and begin to discipline his soul and cleanse it of faults and the stains of sensible attachment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحس<\/bdi>), but then overpowering might (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القهرية<\/bdi>) prevails over him and he turns back from it. If he repents promptly and returns to the path, returning will be easy for him; his success may be hoped for, and his repentance will be accepted. But if he persists in abandoning it until his soul has grown accustomed to idleness, his repentance can no longer be hoped for, and he becomes one of those who have gone astray.<\/p><p>He resembles a vessel from which you have poured out milk or honey and which you have filled with tar. If you promptly pour out the tar, the vessel is easy to wash. But if you leave it until it has stained the vessel, washing it becomes difficult and removing its smell becomes impracticable.<\/p><p>“If he dies having turned back, he will not be gathered in the Hereafter with the people of this companionship, even if a thousand knowers intercede for him. Rather, the consummation of the hidden divine ruse (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المكر<\/bdi>) against him is that his likeness should be cast upon another in the Hereafter, so that those among the people of gnosis who knew him suppose that the other is he. Thus it does not occur to [the knower] to intercede for him.” So said al-Qushayrī.<\/p><p>The glossator says: “What he mentions perhaps has in view the Friend’s encounter with his father, when he meets him covered in darkness and desires to intercede for him. His father is transformed into a filth-smeared <i>dhīkh<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذيخ<\/bdi>)—that is, a pig—and he no longer recognizes him, as in the authentic report. So remember and take heed.” End of quotation. <span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: The gloss “a pig” is the quoted glossator’s; the Cairo editor defines <i>dhīkh<\/i> here as “a male hyena” (I:379, n. 1).]<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N050\">[XIV-N050]<\/a> Success is through God, and He is the guide to the even path.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that someone may enter the path of spiritual training ( التربية ) and begin to discipline his soul and cleanse it of faults and the stains of sensible attachment ( الحس ), but then overpowering might ( القهرية ) prevails over him and he turns back from it. If he repents promptly and returns to the path, returning will be easy for him; his success may be hoped for, and his repentance will be accepted. But if he persists in abandoning it until his soul has grown accustomed to idleness, his repentance can no longer be hoped for, and he becomes one of those who have gone astray. He resembles a vessel from which you have poured out milk or honey and which you have filled with tar. If you promptly pour out the tar, the vessel is easy to wash. But if you leave it until it has stained the vessel, washing it becomes difficult and removing its smell becomes impracticable. “If he dies having turned back, he will not be gathered in the Hereafter with the people of this companionship, even if a thousand knowers intercede for him. Rather, the consummation of the hidden divine ruse ( المكر ) against him is that his likeness should be cast upon another in the Hereafter, so that those among the people of gnosis who knew him suppose that the other is he. Thus it does not occur to [the knower] to intercede for him.” So said al-Qushayrī. The glossator says: “What he mentions perhaps has in view the Friend’s encounter with his father, when he meets him covered in darkness and desires to intercede for him. His father is transformed into a filth-smeared dhīkh ( ذيخ )—that is, a pig—and he no longer recognizes him, as in the authentic report. So remember and take heed.” End of quotation. [Translator’s note: The gloss “a pig” is the quoted glossator’s; the Cairo editor defines dhīkh here as “a male hyena” (I:379, n. 1).] [XIV-N050] Success is through God, and He is the guide to the even path."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then mentioned one who dies in unbelief, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then mentioned one who dies in unbelief, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C379.1","page":379,"ar":"الذيخ - بكسر الذال بعدها ياء ساكنة -: ذكر الضباع. والجمع: أذياخ وذيوخ وذيخة. وأراد بالتلطخ: التلطخ برجيعه أو بالطين.","en":"Dhīkh—with kasra on dhāl followed by an unvocalized yāʾ—means a male hyena. Its plurals are adhyākh, dhuyūkh and dhīkha. “Smeared” means smeared with its excrement or with mud.","anchor":"ذيخاً"}],"english_characters":3678,"arabic_characters":2005},{"id":"q3-91","q":"3:91","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:91 · The ransom that cannot avail and the lost rank of nearness","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 379–380","cairo":"I:379–380","source_page_first":379,"source_page_last":380,"ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا وَماتُوا وَهُمْ كُفَّارٌ فَلَنْ يُقْبَلَ مِنْ أَحَدِهِمْ مِلْءُ الْأَرْضِ ذَهَباً وَلَوِ افْتَدى بِهِ أُولئِكَ لَهُمْ عَذابٌ أَلِيمٌ وَما لَهُمْ مِنْ ناصِرِينَ (91)\nقلت: (ذهباً) : تمييز، و (لو افتدى به) : محمول على المعنى، كأنه قيل: فلن يقبل من أحدهم فدية، ولو افتدى بملء الأرض ذهباً، أو عطف على محذوف، أي: فلن يقبل من أحدهم ملءُ الأرض ذهباً لو تقرب به في الدنيا، ولو افتدى به من العذاب في الآخرة. قاله البيضاوي.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا، واستمروا على كفرهم حتى ماتوا، لن يُقْبَلَ منهم فدية، ولو افتدوا بملء الأرض ذهباً، بل يحصل لهم الإياس من رحمة الله، أُولئِكَ لَهُمْ عَذابٌ أَلِيمٌ فلا ينفعهم فداء منه ولا شفاعة ولا حميم، وَما لَهُمْ مِنْ ناصِرِينَ ينصروهم من عذاب رب العالمين.\nقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «يُجَاءُ بالكَافِر يومَ القيامةِ، فيُقال له: أرأيتَ لو كان لكَ ملءُ الأرض ذهباً- أكنْتَ مفتدِياً به؟\nفيقولُ: نعم، نعم، فيُقال له: قد سُئلْتَ ما هو أيسرُ من ذلك» . يعني: لا إله إلا الله. ثبتنا الله عليها إلى الممات عالمين بها. آمين.\nالإشارة: كل من كفر بطريق أهل الخصوصية، وحرم نفسه من دخول الحضرة القدوسية، واستمر على كفرانه إلى الممات، فلا شك أنه يحصل له الندم وقد زلّت به القدم، لأنه مات مُصرًا على الكبائر وهو لا يشعر، فإذا حشر مع عوام المسلمين، وسَكَن في رَبضِ الجنة مع أهل اليمين، ثم رأى منازل المقربين في أعلى عليين، ندم وتحسر(1) ، وقد غلبه القدر، فلو اشترى المُقَام معهم بملء الأرض ذهباً ما نفعه ذلك، فيمكث في غمّ الحجاب وعذاب القطيعة هنالك، مقطوع عن شهود الأحباب على نعت الكشف والبيان، ممنوع عن الشهود والعيان. وبالله التوفيق.\nولمّا حكم الحق تعالى بأن الفداء لا ينفع يوم القيامة ذكر أفضل ما يفتدى به العبد فى دار الدنيا لأنه ينفع فيها ذلك، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا وَماتُوا وَهُمْ كُفَّارٌ فَلَنْ يُقْبَلَ مِنْ أَحَدِهِمْ مِلْءُ الْأَرْضِ ذَهَباً وَلَوِ افْتَدى بِهِ أُولئِكَ لَهُمْ عَذابٌ أَلِيمٌ وَما لَهُمْ مِنْ ناصِرِينَ (91)","quran_en":"91. Those who disbelieve and die as unbelievers—an earthful of gold will not be accepted from any one of them, even if he offers it as a ransom. For them is a painful punishment, and they will have no helpers.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Specification and an understood ransom","html":"<p>I say: “gold” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذهباً<\/bdi>) is an accusative of specification (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تمييز<\/bdi>). “Even if he offers it as a ransom” is construed according to the sense, as though it had been said: “No ransom will be accepted from any one of them, even if he ransoms himself with an earthful of gold.” Alternatively, the clause is coordinated with something omitted: “An earthful of gold will not be accepted from any one of them, whether he offered it to draw near [to God] in this world or offered it as a ransom from punishment in the Hereafter.” So said al-Bayḍāwī.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “gold” ( ذهباً ) is an accusative of specification ( تمييز ). “Even if he offers it as a ransom” is construed according to the sense, as though it had been said: “No ransom will be accepted from any one of them, even if he ransoms himself with an earthful of gold.” Alternatively, the clause is coordinated with something omitted: “An earthful of gold will not be accepted from any one of them, whether he offered it to draw near [to God] in this world or offered it as a ransom from punishment in the Hereafter.” So said al-Bayḍāwī."},{"type":"exposition","title":"No purchase of release after death","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: those who disbelieve and persist in their unbelief until they die will have no ransom accepted from them, even if they ransom themselves with an earthful of gold. Rather, despair of God’s mercy overtakes them. For them is a painful punishment: neither ransom, intercession nor an intimate friend will avail them against it. They have no helpers to defend them against the punishment of the Lord of the worlds.<\/p><p>The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “The unbeliever will be brought on the Day of Resurrection and asked: ‘Tell Me: had you possessed an earthful of gold, would you have offered it as a ransom?’ He will answer: ‘Yes, yes.’ He will be told: ‘You were asked for something easier than that.’” He means: “There is no god but God.” May God make us steadfast in this word until death, knowing its meaning. Amen. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N051\">[XIV-N051]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: those who disbelieve and persist in their unbelief until they die will have no ransom accepted from them, even if they ransom themselves with an earthful of gold. Rather, despair of God’s mercy overtakes them. For them is a painful punishment: neither ransom, intercession nor an intimate friend will avail them against it. They have no helpers to defend them against the punishment of the Lord of the worlds. The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “The unbeliever will be brought on the Day of Resurrection and asked: ‘Tell Me: had you possessed an earthful of gold, would you have offered it as a ransom?’ He will answer: ‘Yes, yes.’ He will be told: ‘You were asked for something easier than that.’” He means: “There is no god but God.” May God make us steadfast in this word until death, knowing its meaning. Amen. [XIV-N051]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Salvation distinguished from the station of the near ones","html":"<p>Whoever denies the path of those possessed of special spiritual distinction (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الخصوصية<\/bdi>), deprives himself of entry into the Holy Presence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحضرة القدوسية<\/bdi>), and persists in that denial until death will undoubtedly feel regret after his footing has slipped. For he has died persisting in grave sins without realizing it. When he is gathered with ordinary Muslims and dwells in the outskirts of Paradise with the people of the right hand, then sees the dwellings of those brought near in the highest ʿIlliyyūn, he regrets and grieves; but the decree has prevailed over him.<\/p><p>Were he to purchase a station among them with an earthful of gold, it would not avail him. He remains there in the grief of the veil and the pain of separation, cut off from witnessing the beloved ones in unveiling and clarity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الكشف والبيان<\/bdi>), deprived of witnessing and direct vision (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشهود والعيان<\/bdi>). And success is through God. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N052\">[XIV-N052]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever denies the path of those possessed of special spiritual distinction ( أهل الخصوصية ), deprives himself of entry into the Holy Presence ( الحضرة القدوسية ), and persists in that denial until death will undoubtedly feel regret after his footing has slipped. For he has died persisting in grave sins without realizing it. When he is gathered with ordinary Muslims and dwells in the outskirts of Paradise with the people of the right hand, then sees the dwellings of those brought near in the highest ʿIlliyyūn, he regrets and grieves; but the decree has prevailed over him. Were he to purchase a station among them with an earthful of gold, it would not avail him. He remains there in the grief of the veil and the pain of separation, cut off from witnessing the beloved ones in unveiling and clarity ( الكشف والبيان ), deprived of witnessing and direct vision ( الشهود والعيان ). And success is through God. [XIV-N052]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>After the Real Most High had ruled that ransom would not avail on the Day of Resurrection, He mentioned the best thing by which the servant may ransom himself in the abode of this world, where such a ransom does avail. He said:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"After the Real Most High had ruled that ransom would not avail on the Day of Resurrection, He mentioned the best thing by which the servant may ransom himself in the abode of this world, where such a ransom does avail. He said:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C380.1","page":380,"ar":"هذا باعتبار عدم إدراكهم لمنازل المقربين، وإن كان مجرد دخول الجنة فوز ونجاح، قال تعالى: فمن زحزح عن النار وأدخل الجنة فقد فاز.. الآية.","en":"This refers to their failure to attain the stations of those brought near, although admission to Paradise itself is triumph and success. God, exalted is He, says: “Whoever is kept away from the Fire and admitted to Paradise has succeeded ….”","anchor":"ندم وتحسر"}],"english_characters":2573,"arabic_characters":1609},{"id":"q3-92","q":"3:92","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:92 · Spending what one loves: property, service and the gift of oneself","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 380–382","cairo":"I:380–382","source_page_first":380,"source_page_last":382,"ar":"لَنْ تَنالُوا الْبِرَّ حَتَّى تُنْفِقُوا مِمَّا تُحِبُّونَ وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ شَيْءٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ بِهِ عَلِيمٌ (92)\nقلت: البرّ: كمال الطاعة.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: لَنْ تَنالُوا كمال الطاعة والتقرب حَتَّى تُنْفِقُوا مِمَّا تُحِبُّونَ، أو: لن تنالوا برَّ الله، الذي هو الرضى والرضوان، حَتَّى تُنْفِقُوا بعض ما تُحِبُّونَ من المال وغيره، كبذل الجاه في معاونة الناس، إن صحبه الإخلاص، وكبذل البدن فى طاعة الله، وكبذل المهج في سبيل الله. ولمّا نزلت الآية\nجاء أبو طلحة فقال: يا رسول الله، إن أحب أموالي إِليَّ بَيْرُحاء- وهو بستان كان خلف المسجد النبوي- وهو صدقة لله، أرجو برّها وذخرها، فقال له- عليه الصلاة والسلام- «بَخٍ بَخٍ ذَلَكَ مَالٌ رَابحٌ- أو رائح- وَإِنِّي أَرَى أَنْ تَجْعَلَهَا في الأَقْرَبينَ» . فقسمها أبو طلحة في أقاربه.\nوجاء زيدٌ بنُ حَارِثَةَ بفرسٍ كان يُحبها، فقال: هذه في سبيل الله، فحمل عليها رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أسامة ولده، فقال زيد: إنما أردت أن أتصدق بها، فقال- عليه الصلاة السلام-: «إنَّ اللَّهَ تعالى قَدْ قَبِلَهَا» . فدلّ ذلك على أن الصدقة على الأقارب أفضل. وأعتقت امرأةٌ جارية لا تملك غيرها، كانت تحبها، واشترطت عليها أن تقيم معها، فلما عُتِقَتْ، ذهبت، فقال لها عليه الصلاة والسلام: «دعيها فقد حجَبْتِك عن النار» .\nوأمر عمر بن الخطاب بشراء جارية من سبي العراق، فلما جيء بها، ورآها عمرُ أعجبْته غايةً، فقال: إن الله تعالى يقول: لَنْ تَنالُوا الْبِرَّ حَتَّى تُنْفِقُوا مِمَّا تُحِبُّونَ، فأعتقها. وذكر ابن عمر هذه الآية، فلم يجد عنده أحبَّ من جارية كانت عنده، يطؤها فأعتقها، وقال: لولا أني لا أعود في شيء جعلته لله لنكحتها. وكان الربيع يعطي للسائل إذا وقف في بابه السكر، فإذا قيل له في ذلك، قال: إن الربيع يحب السكر.\nثم إن الله- تعالى- يقبل الصدقة من المحبوب أو غيره، ولذلك قال: وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ شَيْءٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ بِهِ عَلِيمٌ فيجازيكم بحسبه.\nالإشارة: ليس للفقير شيء أحبّ إليه من نفسه التي بين جنبيه، بل عند جميع الناس، فمن بذل روحه في مرضاة الله نال رضوان الله ومعرفته، وهو غاية البر، فمن أذل نفسه لله أعزه الله، ومن أفقر نفسه لله أغناه الله، ومن تواضع لله رفعه، فبذل النفس لله هو تقديمها لشيخ التربية يفعل بها ما يشاء، فكل ما يشير به إليه بادر إليه بلا تردد، فمن فعل ذلك فقد نال غاية البر، وأنفق غاية ما يُحب، وكل من بذل نفسه بذل غيرها بالأحرى، إذ ليس أعز منها، وفي ذلك يقول ابن الفارض رضي الله عنه:\nمالى سِوَى رُوحِي، وباذل نَفْسه(1) ... في حُبّ من يَهْوَاهُ ليس بُمُسْرِفِ\nفَلَئِنْ رَضِيتَ بها فقد أسْعَفْتَني ... يا خَيْبَةَ الْمَسْعَى إذا لم تُسْعِفِ\nوقال الشيخ أبو عبد الله القرشي: حقيقة المحبة أن تهب كُلَّكَ لمن أحببته، حتى لا يبقى لك منك شيء. هـ.\nوقال الجنيد رضي الله عنه: لن تنالوا محبة الله حتى تسخُوا بأنفسكم لله. هـ.\nولما قال عليه الصلاة والسلام لليهود: «أنا على ملة إبراهيم» . - كما تقدم- قالوا: كيف تكون على ملة إبراهيم، وأنت تأكل لحوم الإبل وألبانها؟، وكان ذلك حراما على إبراهيم، فأنزل الله تعالى:","quran_ar":"لَنْ تَنالُوا الْبِرَّ حَتَّى تُنْفِقُوا مِمَّا تُحِبُّونَ وَما تُنْفِقُوا مِنْ شَيْءٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ بِهِ عَلِيمٌ (92)","quran_en":"92. You will not attain righteousness until you spend of what you love. Whatever you spend, God knows it.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The meaning of birr","html":"<p>I say: righteousness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البرّ<\/bdi>) is the perfection of obedience.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: righteousness ( البرّ ) is the perfection of obedience."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Wealth, influence, bodily service and life itself","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: you will not attain perfection in obedience and drawing near until you spend of what you love. Or: you will not attain God’s goodness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">برّ الله<\/bdi>), namely His approval and good pleasure (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرضى والرضوان<\/bdi>), until you spend some of what you love, whether wealth or something else. This includes giving the benefit of one’s influence to help people, provided it is accompanied by sincerity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإخلاص<\/bdi>); giving one’s bodily effort in obedience to God; and giving one’s very life in God’s path.<\/p><p>When the verse was revealed,<\/p><p>Abū Ṭalḥa came and said: “Messenger of God, the property dearest to me is Bayruḥāʾ”—a garden behind the Prophet’s mosque—“and it is a charitable gift for God. I hope for its goodness and its stored reward.” He, blessings and peace be upon him, replied: “Excellent, excellent! That is a profitable property”—<i>rābiḥ<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">رابح<\/bdi>), or <i>rāʾiḥ<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">رائح<\/bdi>), literally “going forth,” in the other wording—“and I consider that you should give it to your near relatives.” Abū Ṭalḥa therefore divided it among his relatives. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N053\">[XIV-N053]<\/a><\/p><p>Zayd ibn Ḥāritha brought a horse that he loved and said: “This is for God’s path.” The Messenger of God, blessings and peace be upon him, gave it to Zayd’s son Usāma to ride. Zayd said: “I only intended to give it in charity.” He, blessings and peace be upon him, replied: “God Most High has accepted it.” This indicates that charity to relatives is preferable.<\/p><p>A woman freed an enslaved woman whom she loved and who was her only possession, on condition that she should remain with her. Once freed, the woman departed. He, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “Let her go: she has screened you from the Fire.”<\/p><p>ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb ordered that an enslaved woman from among the captives of Iraq be purchased. When she was brought to him and he saw her, he found her exceedingly pleasing. He said: “God Most High says: ‘You will not attain righteousness until you spend of what you love,’” and he freed her.<\/p><p>Ibn ʿUmar recalled this verse and found nothing he possessed dearer to him than an enslaved woman with whom he had sexual relations. He freed her and said: “Were it not that I do not take back anything I have given for God, I would marry her.” Whenever a beggar stood at al-Rabīʿ’s door, he would give him sugar. When questioned about this, he said: “Al-Rabīʿ loves sugar.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N054\">[XIV-N054]<\/a><\/p><p>God Most High nevertheless accepts charity whether it consists of something loved or something else. Hence He says: “Whatever you spend, God knows it,” and He recompenses you accordingly.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: you will not attain perfection in obedience and drawing near until you spend of what you love. Or: you will not attain God’s goodness ( برّ الله ), namely His approval and good pleasure ( الرضى والرضوان ), until you spend some of what you love, whether wealth or something else. This includes giving the benefit of one’s influence to help people, provided it is accompanied by sincerity ( الإخلاص ); giving one’s bodily effort in obedience to God; and giving one’s very life in God’s path. When the verse was revealed, Abū Ṭalḥa came and said: “Messenger of God, the property dearest to me is Bayruḥāʾ”—a garden behind the Prophet’s mosque—“and it is a charitable gift for God. I hope for its goodness and its stored reward.” He, blessings and peace be upon him, replied: “Excellent, excellent! That is a profitable property”— rābiḥ ( رابح ), or rāʾiḥ ( رائح ), literally “going forth,” in the other wording—“and I consider that you should give it to your near relatives.” Abū Ṭalḥa therefore divided it among his relatives. [XIV-N053] Zayd ibn Ḥāritha brought a horse that he loved and said: “This is for God’s path.” The Messenger of God, blessings and peace be upon him, gave it to Zayd’s son Usāma to ride. Zayd said: “I only intended to give it in charity.” He, blessings and peace be upon him, replied: “God Most High has accepted it.” This indicates that charity to relatives is preferable. A woman freed an enslaved woman whom she loved and who was her only possession, on condition that she should remain with her. Once freed, the woman departed. He, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “Let her go: she has screened you from the Fire.” ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb ordered that an enslaved woman from among the captives of Iraq be purchased. When she was brought to him and he saw her, he found her exceedingly pleasing. He said: “God Most High says: ‘You will not attain righteousness until you spend of what you love,’” and he freed her. Ibn ʿUmar recalled this verse and found nothing he possessed dearer to him than an enslaved woman with whom he had sexual relations. He freed her and said: “Were it not that I do not take back anything I have given for God, I would marry her.” Whenever a beggar stood at al-Rabīʿ’s door, he would give him sugar. When questioned about this, he said: “Al-Rabīʿ loves sugar.” [XIV-N054] God Most High nevertheless accepts charity whether it consists of something loved or something else. Hence He says: “Whatever you spend, God knows it,” and He recompenses you accordingly."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The self as the dearest gift","html":"<p>The spiritual wayfarer (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفقير<\/bdi>) possesses nothing dearer to him than the self within him; indeed, this is true of all people. Whoever gives his spirit in seeking God’s pleasure attains God’s good pleasure and knowledge of Him, which is the utmost righteousness. Whoever humbles his self for God is ennobled by God; whoever makes his self poor for God is enriched by God; and whoever humbles himself before God is raised by Him.<\/p><p>Giving the self to God means presenting it to a shaykh of spiritual training (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شيخ التربية<\/bdi>), who does with it what he wills. Whatever he directs the disciple to do, the disciple hastens to do without hesitation. Whoever does this has attained the utmost righteousness and spent the dearest thing he loves. Whoever gives his self will all the more readily give everything else, for nothing is dearer than it. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N055\">[XIV-N055]<\/a> Concerning this, Ibn al-Fāriḍ, may God be pleased with him, says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">I have nothing but my spirit; one who gives himself<br/>for love of his beloved is no squanderer.<br/>If you are pleased with it, you have granted my desire;<br/>how fruitless the striving if you do not grant it!<\/blockquote><p>Shaykh Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Qurashī said: “The reality of love is to give your entire being to the one you love, until nothing of you remains for you.” End of quotation.<\/p><p>Al-Junayd, may God be pleased with him, said: “You will not attain the love of God until you give yourselves generously to God.” End of quotation.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The spiritual wayfarer ( الفقير ) possesses nothing dearer to him than the self within him; indeed, this is true of all people. Whoever gives his spirit in seeking God’s pleasure attains God’s good pleasure and knowledge of Him, which is the utmost righteousness. Whoever humbles his self for God is ennobled by God; whoever makes his self poor for God is enriched by God; and whoever humbles himself before God is raised by Him. Giving the self to God means presenting it to a shaykh of spiritual training ( شيخ التربية ), who does with it what he wills. Whatever he directs the disciple to do, the disciple hastens to do without hesitation. Whoever does this has attained the utmost righteousness and spent the dearest thing he loves. Whoever gives his self will all the more readily give everything else, for nothing is dearer than it. [XIV-N055] Concerning this, Ibn al-Fāriḍ, may God be pleased with him, says: I have nothing but my spirit; one who gives himself for love of his beloved is no squanderer. If you are pleased with it, you have granted my desire; how fruitless the striving if you do not grant it! Shaykh Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Qurashī said: “The reality of love is to give your entire being to the one you love, until nothing of you remains for you.” End of quotation. Al-Junayd, may God be pleased with him, said: “You will not attain the love of God until you give yourselves generously to God.” End of quotation."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When he, blessings and peace be upon him, told the Jews, “I follow the religious way of Abraham,” as stated above, they said: “How can you follow the way of Abraham when you consume camels’ flesh and milk, although these were forbidden to Abraham?” Thereupon God Most High revealed:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When he, blessings and peace be upon him, told the Jews, “I follow the religious way of Abraham,” as stated above, they said: “How can you follow the way of Abraham when you consume camels’ flesh and milk, although these were forbidden to Abraham?” Thereupon God Most High revealed:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C381.1","page":381,"ar":"في الأصل: روحه.","en":"In the original: “his spirit” [rūḥahu, in place of “himself”].","anchor":"وباذل نَفْسه"}],"english_characters":4331,"arabic_characters":2656},{"id":"q3-93-95","q":"3:93–95","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:93–95 · Israel’s food prohibition, Abraham’s religious way and the discipline of desire","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 382–383","cairo":"I:382–383","source_page_first":382,"source_page_last":383,"ar":"كُلُّ الطَّعامِ كانَ حِلاًّ لِبَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ إِلاَّ ما حَرَّمَ إِسْرائِيلُ عَلى نَفْسِهِ مِنْ قَبْلِ أَنْ تُنَزَّلَ التَّوْراةُ قُلْ فَأْتُوا بِالتَّوْراةِ فَاتْلُوها إِنْ كُنْتُمْ صادِقِينَ (93) فَمَنِ افْتَرى عَلَى اللَّهِ الْكَذِبَ مِنْ بَعْدِ ذلِكَ فَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الظَّالِمُونَ (94) قُلْ صَدَقَ اللَّهُ فَاتَّبِعُوا مِلَّةَ إِبْراهِيمَ حَنِيفاً وَما كانَ مِنَ الْمُشْرِكِينَ (95)\nقلت: (إسرائيل) : هو يعقوب عليه السلام.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: كُلُّ الطَّعامِ كانَ حلالاً على بني إسرائيل، كما كان حلالاً على الأنبياء كلهم، إِلَّا ما حَرَّمَ إِسْرائِيلُ أي: يعقوب، عَلى نَفْسِهِ، كلحوم الإبل وألبانها، قيل: كان به عرق النسا(1) ، فنذر:\nإن شفاه الله لم يأكل أحب الطعام إليه، وكان ذلك أحب الطعام إليه. وقيل: فعل ذلك للتداوي بإشارة الأطباء، فترك ذلك بنوه ولم يحرم عليهم في التوراة، وإنما هو شيء حرموه على أنفسهم.\nفالطعام كله كان حلالاً على بني إسرائيل وعلى الأنبياء كلهم قبل نزول التوراة، فلما نزلت التوراة حرم الله عليهم أشياء من الطيبات لظلمهم وبغيهم، فإن ادعوا أن لحوم الإبل كانت حراماً على إبراهيم، وأن كل ما حرم عليهم كان حراماً على إبراهيم وعلى الأنبياء قبله، فقل لهم: كذبتم فَأْتُوا بِالتَّوْراةِ فَاتْلُوها هل تجدون ذلك فيها؟ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ صادِقِينَ في قولكم: إنَّ كل شيء حرم عليكم كان حراماً على إبراهيم. رُوِيَ: أنه- عليه الصلاة والسلام- لما قال لهم ذلك بهتوا، ولم يجسروا أن يأتوا بالتوراة، فتبين افتراؤهم على الله فَمَنِ افْتَرى عَلَى اللَّهِ الْكَذِبَ\nبزعمه أن الله حرَّم لحوم الإبل وألبانها قبل نزول التوراة، مِنْ بَعْدِ ذلِكَ\nالبيان وإلزامهم الحجة، فَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الظَّالِمُونَ\nالمكابرون بالباطل بعد ما وضَح الحق.\nقُلْ لهم يا محمد: صَدَقَ اللَّهُ فيما أنزل، وكذبتم فيما قلتم، فتبين أن ملة إبراهيم هي الإسلام الذي جاء به محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم فأسلموا، واتبعوا مِلَّةَ إِبْراهِيمَ حَنِيفاً، فإن ملة الإسلام موافقة لملة إبراهيم، أو عينُها، فادخلوا فيه وتخلصوا من اليهودية التي اضطرتكم إلى التحريف والمكابرة، وألزمَتْكُم تحريمَ طيباتٍ أحلها الله لإبراهيم ومن تبعه، وقد خالفتم التوراة التي زعمتم أنكم متمسكون بها، وأشركتم مع الله عزيراً وغيره، وقد كان إبراهيم حنيفاً مسلماً وَما كانَ مِنَ الْمُشْرِكِينَ.\nقال البيضاوي: فيه إشارة إلى أن اتَّبَاعَهُ- أي: إبراهيم- واجبٌ في التوحيد الصرف والاستقامةِ في الدين، والتجنب عن الإفراط والتفريط، وتعريض بشرك اليهود. هـ.\nالإشارة: إذا تحقق للفقير الإخلاص، وحصل على التوحيد الخاص، كان الطعام كله حلالاً له، لأنه يأخذه بالله، ويتناوله من يد الله ويدفعه لله، مع موافقة الشريعة، ولم يغض من أنوار الطريقة بحيث لا يصحبه شرهٌ ولا طمعٌ. وكان عبد الله بن عمر يقول: كُلْ ما شئت، والبس ما شئت، ما أخطأتك خصلتان: سرف أو مخيلة. هـ.\nوإنما امتنعت العباد والزهَّادُ من تناول الشهوات المباحات خوفاً على أنفسهم أن تجمح بهم إلى تناول أسبابهما، فتعطلهم عن العبادة، وكذلك المريدون السائرون، ينبغي لهم التقلل من تناولها لئلا يتعلق قلبهم بشيء منها، فتعطلهم عن السير، وأما الواصلون العارفون، فقد تحقق فناؤهم وبقاؤهم، فهم يأخذون بالله من يد الله، كما تقدم.\nوالحاصل: أن النفس ما دامت لم تُسلم ولم تنقد إلى مشاهدة ربها، وجب جهادها ومخالفتها، فإذا أسلمت وانقادت إلى ربها، وجب الصلح معها وموافقتها فيما يتجلى فيها. والله تعالى أعلم.\nولمّا كانت اليهود لا تحجُّ بيت الله الحرام، الذي بناه خليل الله إبراهيم عليه السلام، مع زعمهم أنهم على ملته، ردَّ الله تعالى عليهم بقوله: إِنَّ أَوَّلَ بَيْتٍ ... الخ، وقيل: تفاخر المسلمون واليهود، فقالت اليهود: بيت المقدس أفضل لأنه مهاجر الأنبياء، وقال المسلمون: الكعبة أفضل لأنه أول بيت وضع في الأرض، أنزل الله تعالى:","quran_ar":"كُلُّ الطَّعامِ كانَ حِلاًّ لِبَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ إِلاَّ ما حَرَّمَ إِسْرائِيلُ عَلى نَفْسِهِ مِنْ قَبْلِ أَنْ تُنَزَّلَ التَّوْراةُ قُلْ فَأْتُوا بِالتَّوْراةِ فَاتْلُوها إِنْ كُنْتُمْ صادِقِينَ (93) فَمَنِ افْتَرى عَلَى اللَّهِ الْكَذِبَ مِنْ بَعْدِ ذلِكَ فَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الظَّالِمُونَ (94) قُلْ صَدَقَ اللَّهُ فَاتَّبِعُوا مِلَّةَ إِبْراهِيمَ حَنِيفاً وَما كانَ مِنَ الْمُشْرِكِينَ (95)","quran_en":"93. All food was lawful to the Children of Israel, except what Israel forbade himself before the Torah was sent down. Say: “Bring the Torah and recite it, if you are truthful.” 94. Whoever, after that, fabricates a lie against God—it is they who are the wrongdoers. 95. Say: “God has spoken the truth; so follow the religious way of Abraham, inclining to the truth. He was not one of those who associate others with God.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Israel is Jacob","html":"<p>I say: “Israel” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إسرائيل<\/bdi>) is Jacob, peace be upon him.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Israel” ( إسرائيل ) is Jacob, peace be upon him."},{"type":"exposition","title":"A personal abstention is not an original universal prohibition","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “All food was lawful to the Children of Israel,” just as it was lawful to all the prophets, “except what Israel”—that is, Jacob—“forbade himself,” such as camel meat and its milk. It is said that he suffered from sciatica (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عرق النسا<\/bdi>) and vowed that, if God healed him, he would no longer eat the food he loved most; camel meat and milk were dearest to him. It is also said that he abstained from them as a treatment at the physicians’ direction. His children continued to abstain, although those foods were not forbidden to them in the Torah: this was something they had forbidden themselves. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N056\">[XIV-N056]<\/a><\/p><p>Thus all kinds of food had been lawful to the Children of Israel, as to all prophets, before the Torah was sent down. When the Torah came down, God prohibited certain good things to them because of their wrongdoing and oppression. If, therefore, they claim that camel meat was forbidden to Abraham, and that everything later forbidden to them had already been forbidden to Abraham and the prophets before him, say to them: “You have lied. Bring the Torah and recite it.” Do you find such a prohibition there? Do so “if you are truthful” in claiming that everything forbidden to you was also forbidden to Abraham.<\/p><p>It is related that when the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, challenged them in this way they were confounded and dared not produce the Torah, whereupon their fabrication against God became manifest. “Whoever fabricates a lie against God” by alleging that God had forbidden camel meat and milk before the Torah was sent down, “after that”—after this explanation and the argument that has been brought home to them—“it is they who are the wrongdoers,” stubbornly maintaining falsehood after the truth has become clear. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N057\">[XIV-N057]<\/a><\/p><p>Say to them, O Muḥammad: “God has spoken the truth” in what He has revealed, and you have lied in what you have asserted. It has thus become clear that Abraham’s religious way (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ملة إبراهيم<\/bdi>) is the submission (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإسلام<\/bdi>) brought by Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him. Submit, then, and follow “the religious way of Abraham, inclining to the truth (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حنيفاً<\/bdi>).” The religious way of Islam agrees with Abraham’s way—or is that way itself. Enter it, and be rid of the Judaism which has led you into alteration and obstinate contention and has obliged you to prohibit good things God made lawful to Abraham and those who followed him. You have departed from the very Torah to which you profess adherence and associated Ezra and others with God, whereas Abraham was one who inclined to the truth, submitted to God, “and was not one of those who associate others with God.”<\/p><p>Al-Bayḍāwī says: In this is an indication that following Abraham is obligatory in pure affirmation of divine unity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوحيد الصرف<\/bdi>), uprightness in religion, and avoidance of excess and deficiency; it also contains an indirect censure of Jewish associationism. End of quotation.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “All food was lawful to the Children of Israel,” just as it was lawful to all the prophets, “except what Israel”—that is, Jacob—“forbade himself,” such as camel meat and its milk. It is said that he suffered from sciatica ( عرق النسا ) and vowed that, if God healed him, he would no longer eat the food he loved most; camel meat and milk were dearest to him. It is also said that he abstained from them as a treatment at the physicians’ direction. His children continued to abstain, although those foods were not forbidden to them in the Torah: this was something they had forbidden themselves. [XIV-N056] Thus all kinds of food had been lawful to the Children of Israel, as to all prophets, before the Torah was sent down. When the Torah came down, God prohibited certain good things to them because of their wrongdoing and oppression. If, therefore, they claim that camel meat was forbidden to Abraham, and that everything later forbidden to them had already been forbidden to Abraham and the prophets before him, say to them: “You have lied. Bring the Torah and recite it.” Do you find such a prohibition there? Do so “if you are truthful” in claiming that everything forbidden to you was also forbidden to Abraham. It is related that when the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, challenged them in this way they were confounded and dared not produce the Torah, whereupon their fabrication against God became manifest. “Whoever fabricates a lie against God” by alleging that God had forbidden camel meat and milk before the Torah was sent down, “after that”—after this explanation and the argument that has been brought home to them—“it is they who are the wrongdoers,” stubbornly maintaining falsehood after the truth has become clear. [XIV-N057] Say to them, O Muḥammad: “God has spoken the truth” in what He has revealed, and you have lied in what you have asserted. It has thus become clear that Abraham’s religious way ( ملة إبراهيم ) is the submission ( الإسلام ) brought by Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him. Submit, then, and follow “the religious way of Abraham, inclining to the truth ( حنيفاً ).” The religious way of Islam agrees with Abraham’s way—or is that way itself. Enter it, and be rid of the Judaism which has led you into alteration and obstinate contention and has obliged you to prohibit good things God made lawful to Abraham and those who followed him. You have departed from the very Torah to which you profess adherence and associated Ezra and others with God, whereas Abraham was one who inclined to the truth, submitted to God, “and was not one of those who associate others with God.” Al-Bayḍāwī says: In this is an indication that following Abraham is obligatory in pure affirmation of divine unity ( التوحيد الصرف ), uprightness in religion, and avoidance of excess and deficiency; it also contains an indirect censure of Jewish associationism. End of quotation."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Permission, watchfulness and the differing states of the wayfarer and the knower","html":"<p>When the spiritual poor one (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفقير<\/bdi>) has attained sincerity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإخلاص<\/bdi>) and realized the particular affirmation of unity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوحيد الخاص<\/bdi>), all food becomes lawful to him, because he receives it through God, takes it from the hand of God, and expends it for God, while remaining in conformity with the revealed Law (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشريعة<\/bdi>). His lights of the spiritual path (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الطريقة<\/bdi>) are not diminished; greed and covetousness do not accompany him. ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar used to say: “Eat what you wish and wear what you wish, so long as two things avoid you: extravagance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سرف<\/bdi>) and vainglory (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مخيلة<\/bdi>).”<\/p><p>Worshippers and ascetics refrained from permissible enjoyments because they feared that their souls would carry them into pursuing the means of those enjoyments and so interrupt their worship. Likewise, beginners travelling the path (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المريدون السائرون<\/bdi>) ought to take little of them, lest their hearts become attached to anything therein and thus be impeded in their journeying (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السير<\/bdi>). Those who have arrived and know God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الواصلون العارفون<\/bdi>), however, have realized their effacement and subsistence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفناء والبقاء<\/bdi>); they take through God, from God’s hand, as stated above.<\/p><p>In sum: so long as the soul has not submitted and yielded to the witnessing of its Lord (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مشاهدة ربها<\/bdi>), it must be struggled against and opposed. Once it has submitted and yielded to its Lord, reconciliation with it is required, and one must agree with it in what becomes manifest within it (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فيما يتجلى فيها<\/bdi>). And God Most High knows best. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N058\">[XIV-N058]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"When the spiritual poor one ( الفقير ) has attained sincerity ( الإخلاص ) and realized the particular affirmation of unity ( التوحيد الخاص ), all food becomes lawful to him, because he receives it through God, takes it from the hand of God, and expends it for God, while remaining in conformity with the revealed Law ( الشريعة ). His lights of the spiritual path ( الطريقة ) are not diminished; greed and covetousness do not accompany him. ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar used to say: “Eat what you wish and wear what you wish, so long as two things avoid you: extravagance ( سرف ) and vainglory ( مخيلة ).” Worshippers and ascetics refrained from permissible enjoyments because they feared that their souls would carry them into pursuing the means of those enjoyments and so interrupt their worship. Likewise, beginners travelling the path ( المريدون السائرون ) ought to take little of them, lest their hearts become attached to anything therein and thus be impeded in their journeying ( السير ). Those who have arrived and know God ( الواصلون العارفون ), however, have realized their effacement and subsistence ( الفناء والبقاء ); they take through God, from God’s hand, as stated above. In sum: so long as the soul has not submitted and yielded to the witnessing of its Lord ( مشاهدة ربها ), it must be struggled against and opposed. Once it has submitted and yielded to its Lord, reconciliation with it is required, and one must agree with it in what becomes manifest within it ( فيما يتجلى فيها ). And God Most High knows best. [XIV-N058]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Since the Jews did not perform pilgrimage to God’s Sacred House, built by Abraham the Friend of God, despite claiming to follow his religious way, God refuted them by saying, “Truly the first House ...” It is also said that the Muslims and the Jews disputed which sanctuary was superior. The Jews said that Jerusalem was superior because it was a place to which prophets migrated; the Muslims said that the Kaʿba was superior because it was the first House placed upon the earth. God Most High revealed:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Since the Jews did not perform pilgrimage to God’s Sacred House, built by Abraham the Friend of God, despite claiming to follow his religious way, God refuted them by saying, “Truly the first House ...” It is also said that the Muslims and the Jews disputed which sanctuary was superior. The Jews said that Jerusalem was superior because it was a place to which prophets migrated; the Muslims said that the Kaʿba was superior because it was the first House placed upon the earth. God Most High revealed:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C382.1","page":382,"ar":"النسا: العصب الوركي، وهو عصب يمتد من الورك إلى الكعب، وهو الذي يأخذه المرض.","en":"The nasā is the sciatic nerve: a nerve extending from the hip to the ankle, which is affected by this ailment.","anchor":"عرق النسا"}],"english_characters":5042,"arabic_characters":3272},{"id":"q3-96-97","q":"3:96–97","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:96–97 · Bakkah, the first House, the obligation of pilgrimage and the inner sanctuary","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 383–385","cairo":"I:383–385","source_page_first":383,"source_page_last":385,"ar":"إِنَّ أَوَّلَ بَيْتٍ وُضِعَ لِلنَّاسِ لَلَّذِي بِبَكَّةَ مُبارَكاً وَهُدىً لِلْعالَمِينَ (96) فِيهِ آياتٌ بَيِّناتٌ مَقامُ إِبْراهِيمَ وَمَنْ دَخَلَهُ كانَ آمِناً وَلِلَّهِ عَلَى النَّاسِ حَجُّ الْبَيْتِ مَنِ اسْتَطاعَ إِلَيْهِ سَبِيلاً وَمَنْ كَفَرَ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ غَنِيٌّ عَنِ الْعالَمِينَ (97)\nقلت: (بكة) : لغة في مكة، والعرب تعاقب بين الباء والميم، تقول: ضربة لازم ولازب، وأغبَطَتْ عليه الحُمِّى وأغْمَطَتْ، وقيل: (مكة) بالميم: اسم للبلد كله، وبكة: اسم لموضع البيت، سميت بذلك لأنها تبك أعناق الجبابرة- أي: تدقها- فما قصدها جبّار قط بسوء إلا قصمه الله. و (مباركاً) : حال من الضمير في المجرور، والعامل فيه الاستقرار، أي: الذي استقر ببكة مباركاً، و (مقام إبراهيم) : مبتدأ، والخبر محذوف، أي: منها مقام إبراهيم، أو بدل من (آيات) ، بدل البعض من الكل، أو عطف بيان، على أن المراد بالآيات: أثر القدم في الصخرة الصّماء، وغوصها فيها إلى الكعبين، وتخصيصُها بهذه المزيَّة من بين الصخور، وإبقاؤه دون سائر آثار الأنبياء، وحفظه مع كثرة أعدائه ألوف سنة، فكان مقام إبراهيم، وإن كان مفرداً، في قوة الجمع، ويدل عليه أنه قرئ (آية) :\nبالتوحيد.\nوقيل: (الآيات) : مقام إبراهيم، وأمْنُ من دخله، فعلى هذا يكون: (ومن دخله) ، عطفاً على (مقام) ، وعلى الأول: استئنافاً. و (حج البيت) مبتدأ، و (لله) : خبر، والفتح لغة الحجاز، والكسر لغة نجد، و (من استطاع) : بدل من (الناس) ، وقيل: فاعل.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ أَوَّلَ بَيْتٍ وُضِعَ في الأرض لِلنَّاسِ للذي استقر بمكة، وبعده بيت المقدس، وبينهما أربعون سنة. بنت الأولَ الملائكة حيالَ البيت المعمور، وأمر اللهُ مَنْ في الأرض أن يطوفوا به كما يطوف أهل السماء بالبيت المعمور، ثم بُنِيَ الثاني. وقيل: بناهما آدم عليه السلام ثم جدَّد الأول إبراهيمُ. حال كونه مُبارَكاً لأنه يتضاعف فيه الحسنات، بكل واحدة مائة ألف، وتكفر فيه السيئات، وتنزل فيه الرحمات، وتتوارد فيه النفحات.\nفِيهِ آياتٌ بَيِّناتٌ واضحات، منها: الحجر الذي هو مَقامُ إِبْراهِيمَ، وهو الذي قام عليه حين رفع القواعد من البيت، فكان كلما طال البناء ارتفع به الحجر في الهواء، حتى أكمل البناء، وغرقت فيه قدمه كأنه طين، ومنها: أن الطير لا تعلوه، ومنها: إهلاك أهل الفيل وردُّ الجبابرة عنه، ونبع زمزم لهاجر بهمز جبريل عليه السلام، وحفر عبد المطلب لها بعد دُثُورها، وأن ماءها ينفع لما شُرب له، وَمَنْ دَخَلَهُ كانَ آمِناً من العقاب في الدارين لدعاء الخليل: رَبِّ اجْعَلْ هَذَا الْبَلَدَ آمِناً، فكان في الجاهلية كل من فعل جريمةً، ثم لجأ إليه لا يُهَاج(1) ولا يعاقب مادام به، وأما في الإسلام فإن الحرم لا يمنع من الحدود ولا من القصاص. وقال أبو حنيفة:\nالحكم باق، وإن من وجب عليه حد أو قصاص فدخل الحرم لا يهاج،، لكن يُضيَّق عليه، فلا يطعم ولا يباع له حتى يخرج.\nقال- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «مَنْ مَاتَ في أحد الحرمين بعثه اللهُ من الآمنين» . وقال أيضاً: «مَنْ حَجَّ هذَا البَيْتَ- فَلَمْ يَرْفُثْ، وَلَمْ يَفْسُقْ، خَرَجَ من ذُنُوبِه كَيَوْمِ وَلَدَتْهُ أُمُّه» .\nوَلِلَّهِ عَلَى النَّاسِ حِجُّ الْبَيْتِ فرض عين على مَنِ اسْتَطاعَ إِلَيْهِ سَبِيلًا بالقدرة على الوصول بصحة البدن، راجلاً أو راكباً مع الزاد المُبلِّغ، والأمن على النفس والمال والدين. وقيل: الاستطاعة: الزاد والراحلة.\nوَمَنْ تركه، وكَفَرَ به، كاليهود والنصارى، وكل من جحده، فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ غَنِيٌّ عنه، وعَنِ حجه، وعن جميع الْعالَمِينَ، أو عبر بالكفر عن الترك، تغليظاً كقوله: «مَنْ تَرَكَ الصَّلاةَ فَقَدْ كَفَر» روى أنه- عليه الصلاة والسلام- لما نزل صدر الآية- جمع أرباب الملل، فخطبهم، وقال: «إن الله كتب عليكم الحج فحجوا» ، فآمنت به ملة واحدة، وكفرت به خمس ملل، فنزل وَمَنْ كَفَرَ ... إلخ.\nالإشارة: قد وضع الله للناس بيتين: أحدهما حسي، وهو الكعبة، والآخر معنوي، وهو القلب، الذي هو بيت الرب، فما دام بيت القلب خالياً من نور الرب اشتاق إلى حج البيت الحسي، فإذا تعمر البيت بنور ساكنه، صار قبلة لغيره، واستغنى عن الالتفات إلى غير نور ربه، بل صار كعبة تطوف به الواردات والأنوار، وتحفه المعارف والعلوم والأسرار، ثم يصير قطب دائرة الأكوان، وتدور عليه من كل جانب ومكان، فكيف يشتاق هذا إلى الكعبة الحسية(1) ، وقد طافت به دائرة الوفود الكونية؟ ولله در الحلاج رضي الله عنه حيث قال:\nيَا لاَئِمِي لا تَلُمْني في هواه فَلَوْ ... عايَنْتَ منه الذي عاينْتَ لم تَلُمِ\nللنَّاسِ حجٍّ ولي حجٍّ إلى سَكَنِي ... تُهْدَى الأضَاحِي، وأُهْدِي مُهْجِتِي ودَمِي\nيطوفُ بالبيت قومٌ لا بجارحةٍ، ... بالله طافوا فأغنَاهم عن الحَرَمِ(2) .\nفي هذا البيت آيات واضحات، وهى إشراق شموس المعارف والأنوار، فى فضاء سماء الأرواح والأسرار، وسطوع أنوار قمر التوحيد في أرض التجريد والتفريد، وظهور أنوار نجوم العلم والحِكم، في أفق سماء ارتفاع الهمم، فهذا كان مقام إبراهيم، إمام الموحدين، فمن دخله كان آمناً من الطرد والبعاد إلى يوم الدين، ومن كفر وجوده فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ غَنِيٌّ عَنِ العالمين.\nقال في الحاشية في قوله: (ومن دخله كان آمنا) ، قيل: وهكذا من دخل في قلب وليٍّ من أوليائه، فإن قلب العارف حرم المراقبات والمشاهدات. هـ. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم رجع الحقّ تعالى إلى معاتبة أهل الكتاب، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّ أَوَّلَ بَيْتٍ وُضِعَ لِلنَّاسِ لَلَّذِي بِبَكَّةَ مُبارَكاً وَهُدىً لِلْعالَمِينَ (96) فِيهِ آياتٌ بَيِّناتٌ مَقامُ إِبْراهِيمَ وَمَنْ دَخَلَهُ كانَ آمِناً وَلِلَّهِ عَلَى النَّاسِ حَجُّ الْبَيْتِ مَنِ اسْتَطاعَ إِلَيْهِ سَبِيلاً وَمَنْ كَفَرَ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ غَنِيٌّ عَنِ الْعالَمِينَ (97)","quran_en":"96. Truly the first House established for humankind was that at Bakkah, blessed and a guidance for the worlds. 97. In it are clear signs—the Station of Abraham. Whoever enters it is safe. Pilgrimage to the House is a duty owed to God by people who are able to find a way to it. As for whoever disbelieves, God is independent of all the worlds.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Bakkah, the circumstantial accusative and the Station of Abraham","html":"<p>I say: “Bakkah” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بكة<\/bdi>) is a dialectal form of “Makkah”; Arabs interchange bāʾ and mīm, as when they say ḍarba lāzim and lāzib for a decisive blow, or aghbaṭat ʿalayhi al-ḥummā and aghmaṭat, concerning a fever. It is also said that “Makkah,” with mīm, names the whole town, whereas “Bakkah” names the location of the House. It was given that name because it “breaks” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تبك<\/bdi>), that is, crushes, the necks of tyrants: no tyrant has intended it evil without God breaking him. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N059\">[XIV-N059]<\/a><\/p><p>“Blessed” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مباركاً<\/bdi>) is a circumstantial accusative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال<\/bdi>) referring to the pronoun in the prepositional expression, with the implied state of being established or located (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستقرار<\/bdi>) as its governor (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العامل<\/bdi>): that is, “the one situated in Bakkah, being blessed.” “The Station of Abraham” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مقام إبراهيم<\/bdi>) may be a subject (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مبتدأ<\/bdi>) whose predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/bdi>) is suppressed: “among them is the Station of Abraham.” Or it may be a part-for-whole substitute (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بدل البعض من الكل<\/bdi>) for “signs” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">آيات<\/bdi>), or an explicative apposition (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عطف بيان<\/bdi>), if the signs intend the impression of his foot in the solid rock, its sinking into it as far as the ankles, the rock’s being distinguished from other rocks by this property, the preservation of this impression rather than other prophets’ traces, and its survival for thousands of years despite his many enemies. Thus the Station of Abraham, though singular, has the force of a plural. Supporting this is a variant reading with the singular “a sign” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">آية<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N060\">[XIV-N060]<\/a><\/p><p>It is also said that the “signs” are the Station of Abraham and the security of whoever enters. On this explanation, “whoever enters it” is coordinated with “the Station”; on the former interpretation it begins a new sentence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استئناف<\/bdi>). “Pilgrimage to the House” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حج البيت<\/bdi>) is a subject (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مبتدأ<\/bdi>), and “belongs to God” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لله<\/bdi>) is its predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/bdi>). The vowel fatḥa [in ḥajj] is the idiom of the Ḥijāz and kasra [ḥijj] the idiom of Najd. “Whoever is able” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">من استطاع<\/bdi>) is an appositional substitute (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بدل<\/bdi>) for “the people” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الناس<\/bdi>); it is also said to be the subject/agent (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فاعل<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N060\">[XIV-N060]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Bakkah” ( بكة ) is a dialectal form of “Makkah”; Arabs interchange bāʾ and mīm, as when they say ḍarba lāzim and lāzib for a decisive blow, or aghbaṭat ʿalayhi al-ḥummā and aghmaṭat, concerning a fever. It is also said that “Makkah,” with mīm, names the whole town, whereas “Bakkah” names the location of the House. It was given that name because it “breaks” ( تبك ), that is, crushes, the necks of tyrants: no tyrant has intended it evil without God breaking him. [XIV-N059] “Blessed” ( مباركاً ) is a circumstantial accusative ( حال ) referring to the pronoun in the prepositional expression, with the implied state of being established or located ( الاستقرار ) as its governor ( العامل ): that is, “the one situated in Bakkah, being blessed.” “The Station of Abraham” ( مقام إبراهيم ) may be a subject ( مبتدأ ) whose predicate ( خبر ) is suppressed: “among them is the Station of Abraham.” Or it may be a part-for-whole substitute ( بدل البعض من الكل ) for “signs” ( آيات ), or an explicative apposition ( عطف بيان ), if the signs intend the impression of his foot in the solid rock, its sinking into it as far as the ankles, the rock’s being distinguished from other rocks by this property, the preservation of this impression rather than other prophets’ traces, and its survival for thousands of years despite his many enemies. Thus the Station of Abraham, though singular, has the force of a plural. Supporting this is a variant reading with the singular “a sign” ( آية ). [XIV-N060] It is also said that the “signs” are the Station of Abraham and the security of whoever enters. On this explanation, “whoever enters it” is coordinated with “the Station”; on the former interpretation it begins a new sentence ( استئناف ). “Pilgrimage to the House” ( حج البيت ) is a subject ( مبتدأ ), and “belongs to God” ( لله ) is its predicate ( خبر ). The vowel fatḥa [in ḥajj] is the idiom of the Ḥijāz and kasra [ḥijj] the idiom of Najd. “Whoever is able” ( من استطاع ) is an appositional substitute ( بدل ) for “the people” ( الناس ); it is also said to be the subject/agent ( فاعل ). [XIV-N060]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The Kaʿba, its signs and reported virtues","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Truly the first House” placed upon the earth “for humankind” was the one established at Makkah, followed by the House of Jerusalem, with forty years between them. The angels built the first facing the Inhabited House (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البيت المعمور<\/bdi>), and God commanded the inhabitants of earth to circumambulate it as the dwellers of heaven circumambulate the Inhabited House; then the second was built. It is also said that Adam built both, and that Abraham later renewed the first. It is “blessed,” because good deeds are multiplied there, each one by a hundred thousand; sins are expiated there, mercies descend, and spiritual gifts (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النفحات<\/bdi>) arrive in succession. <span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: These are explanations and reports transmitted by the commentator, not claims independently established here about construction chronology.]<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N061\">[XIV-N061]<\/a><\/p><p>“In it are clear signs,” conspicuous proofs, including the stone that is “the Station of Abraham,” upon which Abraham stood while he raised the foundations of the House. Whenever the building grew higher, the stone rose with him into the air until he completed the building, and his foot sank into it as though it were clay. Other signs are said to include birds not passing above it, the destruction of the People of the Elephant, the repelling of tyrants, the emergence of Zamzam for Hagar through Gabriel’s striking it, its later rediscovery by ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib after it had been obscured, and the benefit of its water for whatever purpose it is drunk. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N061\">[XIV-N061]<\/a><\/p><p>“Whoever enters it is safe”: from punishment in the two abodes, through the Friend’s prayer, “My Lord, make this land secure” [14:35]. In the pre-Islamic period anyone who committed an offence and then took refuge there was neither attacked nor punished while within it. In Islam, however, the sacred precinct does not bar the prescribed penalties (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحدود<\/bdi>) or retaliation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القصاص<\/bdi>). Abū Ḥanīfa holds that the earlier rule remains: if someone liable to a prescribed penalty or retaliation enters the sacred precinct, he is not attacked, but pressure is applied to him—he is not fed or sold provisions—until he goes out. <span class=\"translator-note\"><\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N062\">[XIV-N062]<\/a><\/p><p>The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “Whoever dies in one of the two sanctuaries will be raised among those who are safe.” He also said: “Whoever performs pilgrimage to this House and neither commits obscenity nor transgresses comes forth from his sins like the day his mother gave birth to him.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N063\">[XIV-N063]<\/a><\/p><p>“Pilgrimage to the House is a duty owed to God by people”: an individual obligation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فرض عين<\/bdi>) upon “whoever is able to find a way to it,” meaning able to reach it with a healthy body, on foot or mounted, having the provisions needed to arrive there, and being secure in person, property and religion. Another explanation of ability (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستطاعة<\/bdi>) is provisions and a mount.<\/p><p>“Whoever” abandons it and “disbelieves” in it—such as Jews, Christians, and anyone who denies its obligation—“God has no need” of that person, his pilgrimage, or any of the worlds; or “disbelief” is used for abandoning it as an intensification, as in the saying: “Whoever abandons prayer has disbelieved.” It is related that when the opening of the verse came down, the Prophet assembled the religious communities and said, “God has prescribed the pilgrimage for you; so perform it.” One community believed him and five denied him; so “whoever disbelieves ...” was revealed. <span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: The report is cited as a report, without independent authentication.]<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N064\">[XIV-N064]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Truly the first House” placed upon the earth “for humankind” was the one established at Makkah, followed by the House of Jerusalem, with forty years between them. The angels built the first facing the Inhabited House ( البيت المعمور ), and God commanded the inhabitants of earth to circumambulate it as the dwellers of heaven circumambulate the Inhabited House; then the second was built. It is also said that Adam built both, and that Abraham later renewed the first. It is “blessed,” because good deeds are multiplied there, each one by a hundred thousand; sins are expiated there, mercies descend, and spiritual gifts ( النفحات ) arrive in succession. [Translator’s note: These are explanations and reports transmitted by the commentator, not claims independently established here about construction chronology.] [XIV-N061] “In it are clear signs,” conspicuous proofs, including the stone that is “the Station of Abraham,” upon which Abraham stood while he raised the foundations of the House. Whenever the building grew higher, the stone rose with him into the air until he completed the building, and his foot sank into it as though it were clay. Other signs are said to include birds not passing above it, the destruction of the People of the Elephant, the repelling of tyrants, the emergence of Zamzam for Hagar through Gabriel’s striking it, its later rediscovery by ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib after it had been obscured, and the benefit of its water for whatever purpose it is drunk. [XIV-N061] “Whoever enters it is safe”: from punishment in the two abodes, through the Friend’s prayer, “My Lord, make this land secure” [14:35]. In the pre-Islamic period anyone who committed an offence and then took refuge there was neither attacked nor punished while within it. In Islam, however, the sacred precinct does not bar the prescribed penalties ( الحدود ) or retaliation ( القصاص ). Abū Ḥanīfa holds that the earlier rule remains: if someone liable to a prescribed penalty or retaliation enters the sacred precinct, he is not attacked, but pressure is applied to him—he is not fed or sold provisions—until he goes out. [XIV-N062] The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “Whoever dies in one of the two sanctuaries will be raised among those who are safe.” He also said: “Whoever performs pilgrimage to this House and neither commits obscenity nor transgresses comes forth from his sins like the day his mother gave birth to him.” [XIV-N063] “Pilgrimage to the House is a duty owed to God by people”: an individual obligation ( فرض عين ) upon “whoever is able to find a way to it,” meaning able to reach it with a healthy body, on foot or mounted, having the provisions needed to arrive there, and being secure in person, property and religion. Another explanation of ability ( الاستطاعة ) is provisions and a mount. “Whoever” abandons it and “disbelieves” in it—such as Jews, Christians, and anyone who denies its obligation—“God has no need” of that person, his pilgrimage, or any of the worlds; or “disbelief” is used for abandoning it as an intensification, as in the saying: “Whoever abandons prayer has disbelieved.” It is related that when the opening of the verse came down, the Prophet assembled the religious communities and said, “God has prescribed the pilgrimage for you; so perform it.” One community believed him and five denied him; so “whoever disbelieves ...” was revealed. [Translator’s note: The report is cited as a report, without independent authentication.] [XIV-N064]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The physical House and the heart as an inward sanctuary","html":"<p>God has established two houses for humankind. One is sensible (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حسي<\/bdi>), the Kaʿba; the other is intelligible or spiritual (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">معنوي<\/bdi>), the heart, which is the House of the Lord (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بيت الرب<\/bdi>). As long as the house of the heart is empty of the Lord’s light, it longs to make pilgrimage to the sensible House. But when the house has been built up with the light of its Indweller, it becomes a direction of orientation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قبلة<\/bdi>) for others and ceases to turn towards anything other than the light of its Lord. Rather, it becomes a Kaʿba around which spiritual visitations (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الواردات<\/bdi>) and lights make their circumambulations, surrounded by gnoses, sciences and secrets. Then it becomes a pole (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قطب<\/bdi>) of the circle of existing things, around which they turn from every side and place. How could such a person long for the sensible Kaʿba when the circle of cosmic visitors revolves around him? <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N065\">[XIV-N065]<\/a><\/p><p>How splendidly al-Ḥallāj, may God be pleased with him, put it:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">O you who blame me, blame me not for love of Him; had you beheld what I beheld, you would not blame.<br/>Other people have a pilgrimage, and I a pilgrimage to my Beloved, in whom I find rest: they offer sacrifices, while I offer my life-blood and blood.<br/>People circumambulate the House without bodily limb: they circumambulate through God, who makes them free of need for the Sanctuary.<\/blockquote><p><span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: The printed <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سكني<\/bdi> (sakanī) is retained. “My Beloved, in whom I find rest” draws on the sense of sakan defined by Ibn Fāris as a beloved in whom one finds repose (Maqāyīs al-lugha, root s-k-n, III:88), also attested in Ibn Manẓūr’s Lisān al-ʿArab. All three lines occur together in Ibn ʿArabī’s Muḥāḍarat al-abrār (II:147, electronic texts inspected), following two other verses about the Beloved. Their order there is pilgrimage, circumambulation, then blame; the circumambulation verse reads “through love” (bi-l-ḥubb) where Cairo has “through God” (bi-llāh). That passage gives the poem without an attribution to al-Ḥallāj. It establishes an earlier literary parallel. Al-Shībī’s <i>Sharḥ Dīwān al-Ḥallāj<\/i>, poem 48, pp. 487–488 (Jamal facsimile inspected), explicitly disputes the attribution: the cited <i>Taqyīd<\/i> introduction describes recitation from al-Ḥallāj, which does not establish composition. Al-Shībī assembles his seven-line text from several recensions; it is not a single continuous witness. The attribution here therefore remains Ibn ʿAjība’s, with this critical disagreement disclosed. The word also permits an echo of a dwelling or resting place; no Arabic emendation is proposed.]<\/span><\/p><p>In this inward house are clear signs: the shining of the suns of gnosis and lights in the open heaven of spirits and secrets; the brightness of the moon of divine unity upon the earth of detachment and singleness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التجريد والتفريد<\/bdi>); and the appearing of the stars of knowledge and wisdom in the horizon of exalted aspirations. This is the station of Abraham, the imam of those who affirm divine unity. Whoever enters it is secure from rejection and remoteness until the Day of Judgment. Whoever denies its existence—God is independent of all the worlds.<\/p><p>It is said in the marginal commentary concerning “Whoever enters it is safe”: the same may be said of whoever enters the heart of one of God’s friends (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ولي<\/bdi>): the heart of the knower (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العارف<\/bdi>) is a sanctuary of watchful awareness and witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المراقبات والمشاهدات<\/bdi>). End of citation. Through God is success, and He is the Guide to the even way.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"God has established two houses for humankind. One is sensible ( حسي ), the Kaʿba; the other is intelligible or spiritual ( معنوي ), the heart, which is the House of the Lord ( بيت الرب ). As long as the house of the heart is empty of the Lord’s light, it longs to make pilgrimage to the sensible House. But when the house has been built up with the light of its Indweller, it becomes a direction of orientation ( قبلة ) for others and ceases to turn towards anything other than the light of its Lord. Rather, it becomes a Kaʿba around which spiritual visitations ( الواردات ) and lights make their circumambulations, surrounded by gnoses, sciences and secrets. Then it becomes a pole ( قطب ) of the circle of existing things, around which they turn from every side and place. How could such a person long for the sensible Kaʿba when the circle of cosmic visitors revolves around him? [XIV-N065] How splendidly al-Ḥallāj, may God be pleased with him, put it: O you who blame me, blame me not for love of Him; had you beheld what I beheld, you would not blame. Other people have a pilgrimage, and I a pilgrimage to my Beloved, in whom I find rest: they offer sacrifices, while I offer my life-blood and blood. People circumambulate the House without bodily limb: they circumambulate through God, who makes them free of need for the Sanctuary. [Translator’s note: The printed سكني (sakanī) is retained. “My Beloved, in whom I find rest” draws on the sense of sakan defined by Ibn Fāris as a beloved in whom one finds repose (Maqāyīs al-lugha, root s-k-n, III:88), also attested in Ibn Manẓūr’s Lisān al-ʿArab. All three lines occur together in Ibn ʿArabī’s Muḥāḍarat al-abrār (II:147, electronic texts inspected), following two other verses about the Beloved. Their order there is pilgrimage, circumambulation, then blame; the circumambulation verse reads “through love” (bi-l-ḥubb) where Cairo has “through God” (bi-llāh). That passage gives the poem without an attribution to al-Ḥallāj. It establishes an earlier literary parallel. Al-Shībī’s Sharḥ Dīwān al-Ḥallāj , poem 48, pp. 487–488 (Jamal facsimile inspected), explicitly disputes the attribution: the cited Taqyīd introduction describes recitation from al-Ḥallāj, which does not establish composition. Al-Shībī assembles his seven-line text from several recensions; it is not a single continuous witness. The attribution here therefore remains Ibn ʿAjība’s, with this critical disagreement disclosed. The word also permits an echo of a dwelling or resting place; no Arabic emendation is proposed.] In this inward house are clear signs: the shining of the suns of gnosis and lights in the open heaven of spirits and secrets; the brightness of the moon of divine unity upon the earth of detachment and singleness ( التجريد والتفريد ); and the appearing of the stars of knowledge and wisdom in the horizon of exalted aspirations. This is the station of Abraham, the imam of those who affirm divine unity. Whoever enters it is secure from rejection and remoteness until the Day of Judgment. Whoever denies its existence—God is independent of all the worlds. It is said in the marginal commentary concerning “Whoever enters it is safe”: the same may be said of whoever enters the heart of one of God’s friends ( ولي ): the heart of the knower ( العارف ) is a sanctuary of watchful awareness and witnessing ( المراقبات والمشاهدات ). End of citation. Through God is success, and He is the Guide to the even way."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High then returned to reproaching the People of the Book, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High then returned to reproaching the People of the Book, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C384.1","page":384,"ar":"أي: لا يقاتل.","en":"That is, he is not fought.","anchor":"لا يُهَاج"},{"id":"C385.1","page":385,"ar":"الصالحون في كل وقت يشتاقون إلى الكعبة المشرفة، فهي قبلتهم في الصلاة. وإليها يكون حج من استطاع منهم. وهي في بلد ولد فيها سيدنا رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم، فكيف لا يشتاقون إليها!!.","en":"The righteous in every age long for the honoured Kaʿba, for it is their direction of prayer. Those among them who are able perform pilgrimage to it. It is in the town where our master, God’s Messenger—blessings and peace be upon him—was born. How, then, could they not long for it?!","anchor":"الكعبة الحسية"},{"id":"C385.2","page":385,"ar":"لو أن الله أغنى أحدا عن الحرم لأغنى سيدنا محمدا صلى الله عليه وسلم.","en":"Had God made anyone free of need for the Sacred Precinct, He would have done so for our master Muḥammad—blessings and peace be upon him.","anchor":"فأغنَاهم عن الحَرَمِ"}],"english_characters":9182,"arabic_characters":4505},{"id":"q3-98-99","q":"3:98–99","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:98–99 · Concealing the way of God and denying the presence of spiritual distinction","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 385–386","cairo":"I:385–386","source_page_first":385,"source_page_last":386,"ar":"قُلْ يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تَكْفُرُونَ بِآياتِ اللَّهِ وَاللَّهُ شَهِيدٌ عَلى ما تَعْمَلُونَ (98) قُلْ يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تَصُدُّونَ عَنْ سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ مَنْ آمَنَ تَبْغُونَها عِوَجاً وَأَنْتُمْ شُهَداءُ وَمَا اللَّهُ بِغافِلٍ عَمَّا تَعْمَلُونَ (99)\nقلت: (تبغونها) : جملة حالية من الواو، أي: لِمَ تصدون عن السبيل باغين لها عوجاً. والعوج- بالكسر- في الدين والقول والعمل-، وبالفتح- في الجدار والحائط وكل شخص قائم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: قُلْ يا محمد في عتابك لليهود: يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تَكْفُرُونَ بِآياتِ اللَّهِ السمعية والعقلية الدالة على صدق نبيه صلى الله عليه وسلم فيما يدعوكم إليه من الإسلام؟ وَاللَّهُ شَهِيدٌ عَلى ما تَعْمَلُونَ مطلع على سرها وجهرها، فيجازيكم عليها، فلا ينفعكم التحريفُ ولا الإسرار.\nيا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تَصُدُّونَ عن طريق الله مَنْ آمَنَ بها، وتبع من جاء بها، تَبْغُونَها عِوَجاً أي:\nطالبين لها اعوجاجاً، بأن تلبسوا على الناس، وتُوهموا أن فيها عوجاً عن الحق، بزعمكم أن التوراة لا تُنْسخ، وبتغيير صفة الرسول- عليه الصلاة والسلام، أو بأن تحرشوا بين المسلمين لتختلف كلمتهم، ويختل أمر دينهم، وأنتم شهداء على أنها حق، وأن الصد عنها ضلال، أو: وأنتم عُدول عند أهل ملتكم، يثقون بأقوالكم، ويستشهدونكم في القضايا، وَمَا اللَّهُ بِغافِلٍ عَمَّا تَعْمَلُونَ فلا بد ان يجازيكم على أعمالكم، فإنه يُمهل ولا يُهمل.\nكرَّر الخطاب والاستفهام مرتين مبالغة في التقريع ونفي العذر، وإشعاراً بأن كل واحد من الأمرين مستقبح في نفسه، مستقل باستجلاب العذاب. ولمَّا كان المنكَر عليهم في الآية الأولى: كفرهم، وهم يجهرون به، ختم بقوله:\nوَاللَّهُ شَهِيدٌ عَلى ما تَعْمَلُونَ، ولمَّا كان في هذه الآية: صدهم المؤمنين عن الإسلام، وكانوا يخفونه ويحتالون فيه، قال: وَمَا اللَّهُ بِغافِلٍ عَمَّا تَعْمَلُونَ. قاله البيضاوي.\nالإشارة: كل من جحد وجود الخصوصية عند أهلها، وصد القاصدين للدخول فيها، استحق هذا العتاب بلا شك ولا ارتياب. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم حذّر المؤمنين من الاستماع لهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"قُلْ يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تَكْفُرُونَ بِآياتِ اللَّهِ وَاللَّهُ شَهِيدٌ عَلى ما تَعْمَلُونَ (98) قُلْ يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تَصُدُّونَ عَنْ سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ مَنْ آمَنَ تَبْغُونَها عِوَجاً وَأَنْتُمْ شُهَداءُ وَمَا اللَّهُ بِغافِلٍ عَمَّا تَعْمَلُونَ (99)","quran_en":"98. Say: “O People of the Book, why do you reject God’s signs when God is witness to what you do?” 99. Say: “O People of the Book, why do you turn those who believe away from God’s path, seeking to make it crooked, while you yourselves are witnesses? God is not heedless of what you do.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The circumstantial clause and two senses of crookedness","html":"<p>I say: “Seeking to make it crooked” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تَبْغُونَها<\/bdi>) is a circumstantial clause (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جملة حالية<\/bdi>) describing the state of the subject represented by the plural wāw; that is, “Why do you obstruct the path while seeking crookedness for it?” The noun ʿiwaj (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عِوَج<\/bdi>), with kasra, applies to religion, speech and action, while ʿawaj (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عَوَج<\/bdi>), with fatḥa, applies to a wall, boundary wall or other upright physical object.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Seeking to make it crooked” ( تَبْغُونَها ) is a circumstantial clause ( جملة حالية ) describing the state of the subject represented by the plural wāw; that is, “Why do you obstruct the path while seeking crookedness for it?” The noun ʿiwaj ( عِوَج ), with kasra, applies to religion, speech and action, while ʿawaj ( عَوَج ), with fatḥa, applies to a wall, boundary wall or other upright physical object."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Open rejection and concealed obstruction","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: Say, O Muḥammad, in reproaching the Jews, “O People of the Book, why do you disbelieve in God’s signs”—the transmitted and rational signs (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السمعية والعقلية<\/bdi>) that show the truthfulness of His Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, in what he summons you to of Islam? “God is witness to what you do”: He knows its concealed and manifest aspects and will requite you for it. Your alterations and secrecy therefore cannot profit you.<\/p><p>“O People of the Book, why do you turn those who believe” in the signs and follow the messenger who brought them “away from God’s path, seeking to make it crooked?” That is, seeking to give the path a distortion: you confuse people and make them imagine that it deviates from the truth by claiming that the Torah cannot be abrogated (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النسخ<\/bdi>), by changing the Messenger’s description, or by stirring up discord among the Muslims so that their word becomes divided and their religious affairs disordered. And “you are witnesses” that this path is true and obstruction of it is error; or you are reputable upright witnesses (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عُدول<\/bdi>) in the eyes of your own community, who trust your testimony and summon you as witnesses in legal cases. “God is not heedless of what you do”: He must surely requite you for your deeds, for He grants respite but does not neglect.<\/p><p>The address and question are repeated twice to heighten rebuke, remove excuse and indicate that each offence is reprehensible in itself and independently attracts punishment. Since the action denounced in the first verse is their unbelief, which they openly declare, it closes with “God is witness to what you do.” Since the second denounces the obstruction of believers from Islam, which they concealed and contrived secretly, it concludes with “God is not heedless of what you do.” Al-Bayḍāwī explains it thus. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N066\">[XIV-N066]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: Say, O Muḥammad, in reproaching the Jews, “O People of the Book, why do you disbelieve in God’s signs”—the transmitted and rational signs ( السمعية والعقلية ) that show the truthfulness of His Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, in what he summons you to of Islam? “God is witness to what you do”: He knows its concealed and manifest aspects and will requite you for it. Your alterations and secrecy therefore cannot profit you. “O People of the Book, why do you turn those who believe” in the signs and follow the messenger who brought them “away from God’s path, seeking to make it crooked?” That is, seeking to give the path a distortion: you confuse people and make them imagine that it deviates from the truth by claiming that the Torah cannot be abrogated ( النسخ ), by changing the Messenger’s description, or by stirring up discord among the Muslims so that their word becomes divided and their religious affairs disordered. And “you are witnesses” that this path is true and obstruction of it is error; or you are reputable upright witnesses ( عُدول ) in the eyes of your own community, who trust your testimony and summon you as witnesses in legal cases. “God is not heedless of what you do”: He must surely requite you for your deeds, for He grants respite but does not neglect. The address and question are repeated twice to heighten rebuke, remove excuse and indicate that each offence is reprehensible in itself and independently attracts punishment. Since the action denounced in the first verse is their unbelief, which they openly declare, it closes with “God is witness to what you do.” Since the second denounces the obstruction of believers from Islam, which they concealed and contrived secretly, it concludes with “God is not heedless of what you do.” Al-Bayḍāwī explains it thus. [XIV-N066]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Recognizing the special spiritual realization of one’s contemporaries","html":"<p>Whoever denies the existence of special spiritual distinction (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخصوصية<\/bdi>) among those who possess it, and obstructs seekers from entering it, undoubtedly merits this reproof. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever denies the existence of special spiritual distinction ( الخصوصية ) among those who possess it, and obstructs seekers from entering it, undoubtedly merits this reproof. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then warned the believers against listening to them, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then warned the believers against listening to them, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2541,"arabic_characters":1792},{"id":"q3-100-102","q":"3:100–102","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:100–102 · Communal discord, holding fast to God and the degrees of Godfearingness","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 386–388","cairo":"I:386–388","source_page_first":386,"source_page_last":388,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِنْ تُطِيعُوا فَرِيقاً مِنَ الَّذِينَ أُوتُوا الْكِتابَ يَرُدُّوكُمْ بَعْدَ إِيمانِكُمْ كافِرِينَ (100) وَكَيْفَ تَكْفُرُونَ وَأَنْتُمْ تُتْلى عَلَيْكُمْ آياتُ اللَّهِ وَفِيكُمْ رَسُولُهُ وَمَنْ يَعْتَصِمْ بِاللَّهِ فَقَدْ هُدِيَ إِلى صِراطٍ مُسْتَقِيمٍ (101) يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ حَقَّ تُقاتِهِ وَلا تَمُوتُنَّ إِلاَّ وَأَنْتُمْ مُسْلِمُونَ (102)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا، الخطاب عامٌ، والمراد: نفر من الأوس والخزرج، إِنْ تُطِيعُوا فَرِيقاً مِنَ الَّذِينَ أُوتُوا الْكِتابَ، وهو شاسُ بن قيس اليهودي، كان شيخاً كبيراً، وكان عظيمَ الكفر شديد الضغن على المسلمين، مرَّ بنفر من الأوس والخزرج، جلوساً يتحدثون، وكان بينهما عداوة في الجاهلية، فغاظه تآلفهم واجتماعهم، وقال: قد اجتمع ملأ بني قَيْلَة بهذه البلاد، فما لنا معهم قرار، فأمر شاباً من اليهود أن يجلس بينهم ويُذكِّرهم يوم بعاث- وهو يوم حرب كان بينهم في الجاهلية- ويُنشدهم بعض ما قيل فيه، وكان الظفرُ في ذلك اليوم للأوس، ففعل، وتنازع القوم وتفاخروا وتغاضبوا، وقالوا: السلاحَ السلاحَ، واجتمع من القبيلتين خلق عظيم، فتوجه إليهم رسول صلّى الله عليه وسلم وأصحابه، فقال: «أبدعْوَى الجَاهِليةِ وأنا بَيْنَ أَظْهُرِكُمْ، بعدَ إذ أكْرَمَكْم اللهُ بالإسْلام، وقَطَعَ به عَنْكُم أمْرَ الجَاهِلِية، وألْفَ بَينكُم؟» فعلموا أنها نزغة من الشيطان وكيدٌ من عدوهم، فَأَلَقَوا السِّلاحَ، واستغفروا، وعانق بعضهم بعضاً، وانصرفوا مع الرسول- صلوات الله عليه وسلامه- فنزلت الآية.\nيا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِنْ تُطِيعُوا فَرِيقاً من اليهود يَرُدُّوكُمْ بَعْدَ إِيمانِكُمْ كافِرِينَ يُبيح بعضكم دماء بعض، كما كنتم في الجاهلية. وَكَيْفَ تَكْفُرُونَ وَأَنْتُمْ تُتْلى عَلَيْكُمْ آياتُ اللَّهِ الدالة على تحريم الدماء والشحناء، وَفِيكُمْ رَسُولُهُ الهادي إلى الصراط المستقيم، وهو إنكار وتعجُّبٌ من كفرهم، بعد اجتماع الأسباب الداعية إلى الإيمان، الصارفة عن الكفران، وإنما خاطبهم الله بنفسه بعد ما أمر الرسولَ بأن يخاطب أهل الكتاب إظهاراً لجلالة قدرهم، وإشعاراً بأنهم الأحقاء بأن يخاطبهم الله ويكلمهم، دون أهل الكتاب لبعدهم عن استحقاق مواجهة الخطاب من الكريم الوهاب. وَمَنْ يَعْتَصِمْ بِاللَّهِ ويتمسك بدينه فَقَدْ هُدِيَ إِلى صِراطٍ مُسْتَقِيمٍ لا عوج فيه وأصل الاعتصام: التمنع.\nثم حَضَّ على التقوى الكاملة والدوام على الإسلام، تنفيراً من الاستماع لمن يخرج عنها، فقال: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ حَقَّ تُقاتِهِ، قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «حق تقاته هو أن يُطاعَ فلا يُعْصَى طرفةَ عين، وأن يُذكر فلا يُنسى، وأن يُشكر فلا يُكفر» . ولما نزلت قالوا: يا رسول الله من يقوى على هذا؟ وشق عليهم، فنزلت: فَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ مَا اسْتَطَعْتُمْ، فنسختها. وقال مُقاتل: معناه: (اتقوا الله حق تقاته، فإن لم تستطيعوا فلا تموتن إلا وأنتم مسلمون) . وعن أنس ابن مالك، قال: (لا يتقي الله عبدٌ حق تقاته حتى يُخْزِن من لسانه) ، وقيل:\nليست بمنسوخة لأنَّ مَنْ جَانَبَ ما نهى الله عنه، وفعل من الطاعة ما استطاع، فقد اتقى الله حق تقاته، فمعناها واحد. وسيأتي تحديد ذلك في الإشارة، إن شاء الله.\nقال البيضاوي: وقيل: معنى (حق تقاته) : أن يُنزه الطاعة عن الالتفات إليها، وعن توقع المجازاة عليها، وفي هذا الأمر تأكيدٌ للنهي عن طاعة أهل الكتاب، وَلا تَمُوتُنَّ إِلَّا وَأَنْتُمْ مُسْلِمُونَ أي: لا تكونوا على حالةٍ سوى الإسلام، إلى أن يدرككم الموت. هـ. أماتنا الله على حسن الختام، مع السلامة والعافية على الدوام.\nالإشارة: كما نهى الله عن طاعة من يرد عن الإيمان، نهى عن طاعة من يصد عن مقام الإحسان، كائناً ما كان، وكيف يرجع عن مقام التحقيق، وقد ظهرت معالم الطريق لمَن سبقت له العناية والتوفيق!. قال بعضهم:\nوالله ما رجع مَن رجع إلا من الطريق، وأما من وصل فلا يرجع أبدًا. إذ لا يمكن أن يرجع من عين اليقين إلى علمِ اليقين، أو من اليقين إلى الظن. ومن أراد الثبات على اليقين فليعتصم بحبل الله المتين، وهو صحبة العارفين، فمن اعتصم بهم فقد اعتصم بالله وَمَنْ يَعْتَصِمْ بِاللَّهِ فَقَدْ هُدِيَ إِلى صِراطٍ مُسْتَقِيمٍ.\nثم خاطب أهل الإحسان فقال: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ حَقَّ تُقاتِهِ بأن تغيبوا عما سواه، ولا تموتن إلا وأنتم منقادون لأحكام الربوبية، قائمون بوظائف العبودية. فهذه الآية خطاب لأهل الإحسان، وفَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ مَا اسْتَطَعْتُمْ: خطاب لأهل الإسلام والإيمان. أو هذه لأهل التجريد، والثانية لأهل الأسباب، أو هذه لأهل الباطن،\nوالثانية لأهل الظاهر، فلكل آية أهل ومحل، فلا نسخ ولا تعارض. وقال الشيخ أبو العباس رضي الله عنه: من أراد الجمع بين الآيتين فليتق الله حق تقاته بباطنه، وليتق الله ما استطاع بظاهره. هـ. وبالله التوفيق.\nثم حضّ الحق جل جلاله على الاجتماع، ونهى عن الفرقة التي رام العدو منهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِنْ تُطِيعُوا فَرِيقاً مِنَ الَّذِينَ أُوتُوا الْكِتابَ يَرُدُّوكُمْ بَعْدَ إِيمانِكُمْ كافِرِينَ (100) وَكَيْفَ تَكْفُرُونَ وَأَنْتُمْ تُتْلى عَلَيْكُمْ آياتُ اللَّهِ وَفِيكُمْ رَسُولُهُ وَمَنْ يَعْتَصِمْ بِاللَّهِ فَقَدْ هُدِيَ إِلى صِراطٍ مُسْتَقِيمٍ (101) يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ حَقَّ تُقاتِهِ وَلا تَمُوتُنَّ إِلاَّ وَأَنْتُمْ مُسْلِمُونَ (102)","quran_en":"100. O you who believe, if you obey a group of those who were given the Book, they will turn you back into disbelievers after your faith. 101. How could you disbelieve while God’s signs are recited to you and His Messenger is among you? Whoever holds fast to God has indeed been guided to a straight path. 102. O you who believe, be mindful of God as He is truly worthy of being regarded, and do not die except in submission to Him.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"An attempt to revive the feud between Aws and Khazraj","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “O you who believe.” The address is general but those intended are a group of the Aws and Khazraj. “If you obey a group of those who were given the Book”—namely Shās ibn Qays the Jew. He was an elderly man deeply entrenched in unbelief and intensely resentful of the Muslims. He passed some men of the Aws and Khazraj sitting together in conversation; there had formerly been enmity between the two clans during the pre-Islamic period (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجاهلية<\/bdi>). Their affection and gathering angered him. He said: “The leading men of the Banū Qayla have gathered here; if they remain united we shall have no place among them.” He commanded a Jewish youth to sit with them, remind them of the Day of Buʿāth—a battle between them before Islam—and recite some of the verses composed about it. The Aws had prevailed on that day. The youth did as instructed; they began to dispute and boast, then became angry and shouted, “To arms! To arms!” Many from both clans assembled.<\/p><p>The Messenger of God, blessings and peace be upon him, went out to them with his companions and said: “Do you call with the call of the Age of Ignorance while I am here among you, after God has honoured you with Islam, cut off from you the affair of ignorance, and brought you together?” They recognized it as an incitement from Satan and a stratagem of their enemy. They threw down their weapons, sought forgiveness, embraced one another, and returned with the Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him. Then the verse was revealed. <span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: This is the occasion-of-revelation narrative transmitted by Ibn ʿAjība; its historical details require separate assessment.]<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N067\">[XIV-N067]<\/a><\/p><p>“O you who believe, if you obey a group” of Jews, “they will turn you back into disbelievers after your faith,” making one another’s blood licit again as in the Age of Ignorance. “How could you disbelieve while God’s signs”—which declare bloodshed and rancour forbidden—“are recited to you, and His Messenger is among you,” guiding you to the straight path? This is both repudiation and astonishment at the possibility of unbelief after all the causes inviting them to faith and diverting them from unbelief have been assembled. God now addresses the believers directly, after commanding the Messenger to address the People of the Book, in order to make manifest their exalted worth: they are more deserving that God should address and speak to them, unlike those distant from receiving the Generous Bestower’s direct address. “Whoever holds fast to God,” cleaving to His religion, “has indeed been guided to a straight path” without deviation. The basic sense of holding fast (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاعتصام<\/bdi>) is taking protection against something (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التمنع<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “O you who believe.” The address is general but those intended are a group of the Aws and Khazraj. “If you obey a group of those who were given the Book”—namely Shās ibn Qays the Jew. He was an elderly man deeply entrenched in unbelief and intensely resentful of the Muslims. He passed some men of the Aws and Khazraj sitting together in conversation; there had formerly been enmity between the two clans during the pre-Islamic period ( الجاهلية ). Their affection and gathering angered him. He said: “The leading men of the Banū Qayla have gathered here; if they remain united we shall have no place among them.” He commanded a Jewish youth to sit with them, remind them of the Day of Buʿāth—a battle between them before Islam—and recite some of the verses composed about it. The Aws had prevailed on that day. The youth did as instructed; they began to dispute and boast, then became angry and shouted, “To arms! To arms!” Many from both clans assembled. The Messenger of God, blessings and peace be upon him, went out to them with his companions and said: “Do you call with the call of the Age of Ignorance while I am here among you, after God has honoured you with Islam, cut off from you the affair of ignorance, and brought you together?” They recognized it as an incitement from Satan and a stratagem of their enemy. They threw down their weapons, sought forgiveness, embraced one another, and returned with the Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him. Then the verse was revealed. [Translator’s note: This is the occasion-of-revelation narrative transmitted by Ibn ʿAjība; its historical details require separate assessment.] [XIV-N067] “O you who believe, if you obey a group” of Jews, “they will turn you back into disbelievers after your faith,” making one another’s blood licit again as in the Age of Ignorance. “How could you disbelieve while God’s signs”—which declare bloodshed and rancour forbidden—“are recited to you, and His Messenger is among you,” guiding you to the straight path? This is both repudiation and astonishment at the possibility of unbelief after all the causes inviting them to faith and diverting them from unbelief have been assembled. God now addresses the believers directly, after commanding the Messenger to address the People of the Book, in order to make manifest their exalted worth: they are more deserving that God should address and speak to them, unlike those distant from receiving the Generous Bestower’s direct address. “Whoever holds fast to God,” cleaving to His religion, “has indeed been guided to a straight path” without deviation. The basic sense of holding fast ( الاعتصام ) is taking protection against something ( التمنع )."},{"type":"exposition","title":"“As He should be feared” and the question of abrogation","html":"<p>God then urged perfect Godfearingness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التقوى الكاملة<\/bdi>) and constancy in submission, discouraging them from listening to anyone who would remove them from these. “O you who believe, be mindful of God as He is truly worthy of being regarded.” The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is reported to have said: “His true due is that He should be obeyed and not disobeyed for the blink of an eye, remembered and not forgotten, thanked and not denied.” When the verse came down, they asked, “Messenger of God, who can bear this?” It was difficult for them; then “Be mindful of God as far as you are able” [64:16] was revealed and abrogated it, according to this explanation. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N068\">[XIV-N068]<\/a><\/p><p>Muqātil said its meaning is: “Be mindful of God as He truly deserves; if you cannot, then do not die except as Muslims.” From Anas ibn Mālik it is related: “No servant is mindful of God as He truly deserves until he stores up [restrains] his tongue.” Others say that it is not abrogated: whoever avoids what God has forbidden and performs of obedience whatever he is able has been mindful of God as He truly deserves; the meaning of the two verses is therefore one. We shall explain its exact application in the spiritual allusion, God willing.<\/p><p>Al-Bayḍāwī says: It is also said that the meaning of “as He truly deserves” is to purify obedience of looking at one’s obedience and of expecting recompense for it. The command reinforces the prohibition on obeying the People of the Book. “And do not die except while you are Muslims” means: remain in no state but Islam until death overtakes you. End of quotation. May God cause us to die with a good end, in abiding safety and wellbeing.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"God then urged perfect Godfearingness ( التقوى الكاملة ) and constancy in submission, discouraging them from listening to anyone who would remove them from these. “O you who believe, be mindful of God as He is truly worthy of being regarded.” The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is reported to have said: “His true due is that He should be obeyed and not disobeyed for the blink of an eye, remembered and not forgotten, thanked and not denied.” When the verse came down, they asked, “Messenger of God, who can bear this?” It was difficult for them; then “Be mindful of God as far as you are able” [64:16] was revealed and abrogated it, according to this explanation. [XIV-N068] Muqātil said its meaning is: “Be mindful of God as He truly deserves; if you cannot, then do not die except as Muslims.” From Anas ibn Mālik it is related: “No servant is mindful of God as He truly deserves until he stores up [restrains] his tongue.” Others say that it is not abrogated: whoever avoids what God has forbidden and performs of obedience whatever he is able has been mindful of God as He truly deserves; the meaning of the two verses is therefore one. We shall explain its exact application in the spiritual allusion, God willing. Al-Bayḍāwī says: It is also said that the meaning of “as He truly deserves” is to purify obedience of looking at one’s obedience and of expecting recompense for it. The command reinforces the prohibition on obeying the People of the Book. “And do not die except while you are Muslims” means: remain in no state but Islam until death overtakes you. End of quotation. May God cause us to die with a good end, in abiding safety and wellbeing."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Holding fast through companionship and the certainty of realization","html":"<p>Just as God forbade obedience to one who turns people back from faith (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإيمان<\/bdi>), He forbade obedience to anyone who diverts them from the station of spiritual excellence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مقام الإحسان<\/bdi>), whoever that person might be. How can anyone return from the station of realized certainty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التحقيق<\/bdi>), once the signs of the path have appeared to one for whom divine care and success were decreed? Someone said: “By God, no one turns back except someone who was still on the road. As for one who has arrived, he never turns back,” for it is impossible to return from the eye of certainty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عين اليقين<\/bdi>) to the knowledge of certainty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">علم اليقين<\/bdi>), or from certainty to conjecture (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الظن<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>Whoever desires permanence in certainty should hold fast to God’s strong cord, which is companionship with the knowers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صحبة العارفين<\/bdi>). Whoever holds fast to them has held fast to God: “Whoever holds fast to God has indeed been guided to a straight path.”<\/p><p>Then He addressed the people of spiritual excellence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الإحسان<\/bdi>): “O you who believe, be mindful of God as He truly deserves,” by being absent to everything other than Him; “and do not die except while you are Muslims,” yielding to the determinations of Lordship (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أحكام الربوبية<\/bdi>) and fulfilling the functions of servanthood (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وظائف العبودية<\/bdi>). This verse, then, is addressed to the people of iḥsān, while “Be mindful of God as far as you are able” [64:16] is addressed to the people of Islam and faith. Or the former is for the people of detachment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل التجريد<\/bdi>) and the latter for those occupied with secondary causes (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الأسباب<\/bdi>); or the former is for the inward people (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الباطن<\/bdi>) and the latter for the outward people (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الظاهر<\/bdi>). Each verse has its people and its proper place: there is therefore no abrogation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نسخ<\/bdi>) and no contradiction (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تعارض<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N068\">[XIV-N068]<\/a><\/p><p>Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās, may God be pleased with him, said: “Whoever wishes to reconcile the two verses should be mindful of God as He truly deserves inwardly (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بباطنه<\/bdi>), and mindful of Him as far as possible outwardly (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بظاهره<\/bdi>).” End of quotation. And success is through God.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Just as God forbade obedience to one who turns people back from faith ( الإيمان ), He forbade obedience to anyone who diverts them from the station of spiritual excellence ( مقام الإحسان ), whoever that person might be. How can anyone return from the station of realized certainty ( التحقيق ), once the signs of the path have appeared to one for whom divine care and success were decreed? Someone said: “By God, no one turns back except someone who was still on the road. As for one who has arrived, he never turns back,” for it is impossible to return from the eye of certainty ( عين اليقين ) to the knowledge of certainty ( علم اليقين ), or from certainty to conjecture ( الظن ). Whoever desires permanence in certainty should hold fast to God’s strong cord, which is companionship with the knowers ( صحبة العارفين ). Whoever holds fast to them has held fast to God: “Whoever holds fast to God has indeed been guided to a straight path.” Then He addressed the people of spiritual excellence ( أهل الإحسان ): “O you who believe, be mindful of God as He truly deserves,” by being absent to everything other than Him; “and do not die except while you are Muslims,” yielding to the determinations of Lordship ( أحكام الربوبية ) and fulfilling the functions of servanthood ( وظائف العبودية ). This verse, then, is addressed to the people of iḥsān, while “Be mindful of God as far as you are able” [64:16] is addressed to the people of Islam and faith. Or the former is for the people of detachment ( أهل التجريد ) and the latter for those occupied with secondary causes ( أهل الأسباب ); or the former is for the inward people ( أهل الباطن ) and the latter for the outward people ( أهل الظاهر ). Each verse has its people and its proper place: there is therefore no abrogation ( نسخ ) and no contradiction ( تعارض ). [XIV-N068] Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās, may God be pleased with him, said: “Whoever wishes to reconcile the two verses should be mindful of God as He truly deserves inwardly ( بباطنه ), and mindful of Him as far as possible outwardly ( بظاهره ).” End of quotation. And success is through God."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, then urged unity and forbade the division that their enemy had sought among them, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, then urged unity and forbade the division that their enemy had sought among them, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":6621,"arabic_characters":4218},{"id":"q3-103","q":"3:103","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:103 · The rope of God, the reconciliation of hearts and the unity of the spiritual path","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 388–389","cairo":"I:388–389","source_page_first":388,"source_page_last":389,"ar":"وَاعْتَصِمُوا بِحَبْلِ اللَّهِ جَمِيعاً وَلا تَفَرَّقُوا وَاذْكُرُوا نِعْمَتَ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ إِذْ كُنْتُمْ أَعْداءً فَأَلَّفَ بَيْنَ قُلُوبِكُمْ فَأَصْبَحْتُمْ بِنِعْمَتِهِ إِخْواناً وَكُنْتُمْ عَلى شَفا حُفْرَةٍ مِنَ النَّارِ فَأَنْقَذَكُمْ مِنْها كَذلِكَ يُبَيِّنُ اللَّهُ لَكُمْ آياتِهِ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَهْتَدُونَ (103)\nقلت: أصل الحبل في اللغة: السبب المُوصِّل إلى البغية، سمى به الإيمان أو القرآن لأنه يُوصل إلى السعادة السرمدية، و (شفا حفرة) أي: طرفها، وأصله: (شفو) ، فقلبت ألفاً في المذكر، وحذفت في المؤنث، فقالوا: شفة.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَاعْتَصِمُوا أي: تمسكوا يا معشر المسلمين بِحَبْلِ اللَّهِ أي: الإيمان، أو كتاب الله، لقوله عليه الصلاة والسلام: «إنَّ هذا القرآن هو حَبْلُ الله المّتِين، وهو النورُ المُبِينُ، والشِّفَاءُ النافِعُ، عِصْمَةٌ لِمَنْ تَمَسَّكَ به ... » . الحديث. حال كونكم جَمِيعاً أي: مجتمعين عليه، وَلا تَفَرَّقُوا تفرقكم الجاهلي، أو لا تفرقوا عن الحق بوقوع الاختلاف بينكم كأهل الكتاب. قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «إنَّ بَني إسْرائيلَ افتَرقَتْ على إحدى وسبعين فرقة، وإنَّ أُمَّتِي سَتَفْتَرِقُ على ثِنتَيْنِ وسَبْعِينَ فِرْقَةً، كُلّها في النار إلا واحدة، فقيل: يا رسول الله، ما هذه الواحدة؟ فقبض يده وقال: الجَمَاعَةُ، ثمَّ قرأ: وَاعْتَصِمُوا بِحَبْلِ اللَّهِ جَمِيعاً وَلا تَفَرَّقُوا.\nوَاذْكُرُوا نِعْمَتَ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ، التي من جملتها الهداية للإسلام المؤدِّي إلى التآلف وزوال الغِلِّ، إِذْ كُنْتُمْ أَعْداءً في الجاهلية، يقتل بعضُكم بعضاً، فَأَلَّفَ بَيْنَ قُلُوبِكُمْ بالإسلام، فَأَصْبَحْتُمْ بِنِعْمَتِهِ إِخْواناً متحابين مجتمعين على الأخوة فى الله. قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «لا تَحَاسَدُوا، ولا تَبَاغَضُوا، ولا تَدَابَرُوا، وكُونُوا عبادَ الله إخوانا، المسلم أخو المسلمُ لا يَظْلِمُه ولا يَخْذُلُه» . الحديث. رُوي أن الأوس والخزرج كانوا أخَوَيْن لأبوين، فوقع بين أولادهما العداوة، وتطاولت الحرب بينهما مائة وعشرين سنة، حتى أطفأها الله بالإسلام، وألف بينهم برسوله عليه الصلاة والسلام- فنزلت فيهم هذه الآية.\nثم قال لهم: وَكُنْتُمْ عَلى شَفا حُفْرَةٍ مِنَ النَّارِ أي: مُشرفين على نار جهنم، إذ لو أدرككم الموت لوقعتم في النار، فَأَنْقَذَكُمْ الله مِنْها برسوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام-. رُوِيَ أن أعرابيّاً سمع ابن عباس يقرأ هذه الآية، فقال الأعرابي: والله ما أنقذهم منها وهو يريد أن يوقعهم فيها، فقال ابن عباس رضي الله عنه خذوها من غير فقيه. هـ. كَذلِكَ يُبَيِّنُ اللَّهُ لَكُمْ آياتِهِ أي: مثل هذا التبيين يُبَيِّنُ اللَّهُ لَكُمْ آياتِهِ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَهْتَدُونَ إلى الخير، وتزيدون ثباتاً فيه.\nالإشارة: المذاهب كلها وقع فيها الاختلاف والتفرق في الأصول والفروع، إلا مذاهب الصوفية فكلها متفقة بداية ونهاية، إذ بدايتهم مجاهدة، ونهايتهم مشاهدة، وإلى ذلك أشار في المباحث، حيث قال:\nمذاهبُ الناسِ على اخْتلاف ... ومَذْهَبُ القَوْمِ على ائْتِلاَف\nوإن وقع الاختلاف في بعض الطرق الموصلة إلى المقصود، فقد اتفقت في النهاية، بخلاف أهل الظاهر، لا تجدهم يتفقون إلا في مسائل قليلة، لأن مذهبهم مبني على غلبة الظن، ومذهب القوم مبني على التحقيق ذوقاً وكشفاً، وكذلك ائتلفت أيضاً قلوبهم وأرواحهم، إذ كلهم متخلقون بالشفقة والرأفة والمودة والألفة والصفا لأنهم دخلوا الجنة- أعني جنة المعارف- فتخلقوا بأخلاق أهل الجنة، قال تعالى: وَنَزَعْنا مَا فِي صُدُورِهِمْ مِنْ غِلٍّ إِخْواناً عَلى سُرُرٍ مُتَقابِلِينَ، فيقال لهم بعد الفتح: واذكروا نعمةَ الله عليكم، إذ كنتم أعداء قبل اتصالكم بالطبيب، فألف بين قلوبكم، فأصبحتم بنعمته أخواناً متحابين، وكنتم على شفا حفرة من نار القطيعة والحجاب فَأَنْقَذَكُمْ مِنْها. مثل هذا البيان يوضح الله آياته، أي: تجلياته، لعلكم تهتدون إلى مشاهدة ذاته في أنوار صفاته. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم أمرهم الحق تعالى بالأمر بالمعروف والنهي عن المنكر، ووجه اتصاله بما قبله: أنهم سكتوا حين حرّش بينهم اليهود حتى هموا بالقتال، ولم يأمرهم أحد بالإمساك عنه، فحذّرهم الله من نزغته، وحضّهم على الاجتماع، وأمرهم بالأمر بالمعروف والنهي عن المنكر إذا رأوا شيئا من ذلك، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَاعْتَصِمُوا بِحَبْلِ اللَّهِ جَمِيعاً وَلا تَفَرَّقُوا وَاذْكُرُوا نِعْمَتَ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ إِذْ كُنْتُمْ أَعْداءً فَأَلَّفَ بَيْنَ قُلُوبِكُمْ فَأَصْبَحْتُمْ بِنِعْمَتِهِ إِخْواناً وَكُنْتُمْ عَلى شَفا حُفْرَةٍ مِنَ النَّارِ فَأَنْقَذَكُمْ مِنْها كَذلِكَ يُبَيِّنُ اللَّهُ لَكُمْ آياتِهِ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَهْتَدُونَ (103)","quran_en":"103. Hold fast, all together, to the rope of God, and do not divide. Remember God’s blessing upon you: you were enemies, and He joined your hearts, so that through His blessing you became brothers. You were upon the brink of a pit of fire, and He rescued you from it. Thus God makes His signs clear to you, that you may be guided.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The rope as a means of attaining the end","html":"<p>I say: The original sense of “rope” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحبل<\/bdi>) in language is a means (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السبب<\/bdi>) by which one reaches the object sought; faith or the Qurʾān is so named because it leads to unending happiness. “The edge of a pit” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شفا حفرة<\/bdi>) means its rim. Its original form is shafw (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شفو<\/bdi>), the wāw being changed into alif in the masculine and omitted in the feminine, whence the word shafa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شفة<\/bdi>), “lip.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: The original sense of “rope” ( الحبل ) in language is a means ( السبب ) by which one reaches the object sought; faith or the Qurʾān is so named because it leads to unending happiness. “The edge of a pit” ( شفا حفرة ) means its rim. Its original form is shafw ( شفو ), the wāw being changed into alif in the masculine and omitted in the feminine, whence the word shafa ( شفة ), “lip.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"God’s cord and the reversal of inherited enmity","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Hold fast,” O company of Muslims, that is, cling “to the rope of God,” meaning faith or God’s Book, according to the Prophet’s saying: “This Qurʾān is God’s strong rope, the manifest light, the beneficial healing, and a protection for whoever holds fast to it ...” and the remainder of the report. Do so “all together,” united upon it; “and do not divide” as you were divided in the Age of Ignorance, or do not part from the truth through discord among yourselves as the People of the Book did. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N069\">[XIV-N069]<\/a><\/p><p>The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said, as cited here: “The Children of Israel split into seventy-one sects, and my community will split into seventy-two sects, all of them in the Fire except one.” Asked, “Messenger of God, which is the one?”, he clasped his hand and said: “The community (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجماعة<\/bdi>).” Then he recited: “Hold fast, all together, to the rope of God, and do not divide.” <span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: The numbers and wording here must be kept distinct from Ibn ʿAjība’s other version of the report in his very next section.]<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N069\">[XIV-N069]<\/a><\/p><p>“Remember God’s blessing upon you,” one of its forms being guidance to Islam, which brings about affection and removes rancour, “when you were enemies” in the Age of Ignorance, killing one another, “and He joined your hearts” through Islam, “so that by His blessing you became brothers,” loving one another and united in brotherhood for God’s sake. The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “Do not envy one another, hate one another or turn your backs on one another; be, servants of God, brothers. A Muslim is the brother of a Muslim; he does not wrong him or abandon him.” The remainder of the report follows. It is related that Aws and Khazraj were originally two full brothers whose descendants became enemies and warred for a hundred and twenty years, until God extinguished this through Islam and united them by His Messenger; the verse was revealed about them. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N070\">[XIV-N070]<\/a><\/p><p>He then says to them: “You were upon the brink of a pit of fire,” almost falling into Hell; had death overtaken you, you would have fallen in. “And He rescued you from it,” through His Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him. It is related that a Bedouin heard Ibn ʿAbbās reciting this verse and said: “By God, He did not rescue them from it in order to cast them into it.” Ibn ʿAbbās replied: “Take this from someone who is not a jurist.” End of report. “Thus God makes His signs clear to you,” in such a manner He explains them, “that you may be guided” to the good and increase in steadfastness upon it. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N070\">[XIV-N070]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Hold fast,” O company of Muslims, that is, cling “to the rope of God,” meaning faith or God’s Book, according to the Prophet’s saying: “This Qurʾān is God’s strong rope, the manifest light, the beneficial healing, and a protection for whoever holds fast to it ...” and the remainder of the report. Do so “all together,” united upon it; “and do not divide” as you were divided in the Age of Ignorance, or do not part from the truth through discord among yourselves as the People of the Book did. [XIV-N069] The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said, as cited here: “The Children of Israel split into seventy-one sects, and my community will split into seventy-two sects, all of them in the Fire except one.” Asked, “Messenger of God, which is the one?”, he clasped his hand and said: “The community ( الجماعة ).” Then he recited: “Hold fast, all together, to the rope of God, and do not divide.” [Translator’s note: The numbers and wording here must be kept distinct from Ibn ʿAjība’s other version of the report in his very next section.] [XIV-N069] “Remember God’s blessing upon you,” one of its forms being guidance to Islam, which brings about affection and removes rancour, “when you were enemies” in the Age of Ignorance, killing one another, “and He joined your hearts” through Islam, “so that by His blessing you became brothers,” loving one another and united in brotherhood for God’s sake. The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “Do not envy one another, hate one another or turn your backs on one another; be, servants of God, brothers. A Muslim is the brother of a Muslim; he does not wrong him or abandon him.” The remainder of the report follows. It is related that Aws and Khazraj were originally two full brothers whose descendants became enemies and warred for a hundred and twenty years, until God extinguished this through Islam and united them by His Messenger; the verse was revealed about them. [XIV-N070] He then says to them: “You were upon the brink of a pit of fire,” almost falling into Hell; had death overtaken you, you would have fallen in. “And He rescued you from it,” through His Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him. It is related that a Bedouin heard Ibn ʿAbbās reciting this verse and said: “By God, He did not rescue them from it in order to cast them into it.” Ibn ʿAbbās replied: “Take this from someone who is not a jurist.” End of report. “Thus God makes His signs clear to you,” in such a manner He explains them, “that you may be guided” to the good and increase in steadfastness upon it. [XIV-N070]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Concord among the paths of realization and the healing of the heart","html":"<p>All schools (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المذاهب<\/bdi>) have experienced disagreement and division in fundamentals and branches, save the ways of the Sufis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مذاهب الصوفية<\/bdi>): these, says Ibn ʿAjība, agree in their beginning and their end, for their beginning is spiritual struggle (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المجاهدة<\/bdi>) and their end is witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المشاهدة<\/bdi>). The author of <i>al-Mabāḥith<\/i> alludes to this:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">The schools of people stand in disagreement;<br/>The school of the Folk stands in concord.<\/blockquote><p>Even if they disagree over particular ways (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الطرق<\/bdi>) of reaching the aim, they agree concerning the end. This is unlike the people of outward inquiry (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الظاهر<\/bdi>), whom, in Ibn ʿAjība’s characterization, one finds agreeing only on a few questions, for their method is built upon the preponderance of conjecture (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غلبة الظن<\/bdi>), while the method of the Folk (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القوم<\/bdi>) is grounded in realization through tasting and unveiling (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التحقيق ذوقاً وكشفاً<\/bdi>). Their hearts and spirits, likewise, are united: all are formed by compassion, tenderness, affection, harmony and purity, because they have entered Paradise—the Paradise of gnoses (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جنة المعارف<\/bdi>)—and adopted the character traits of the inhabitants of Paradise. God says: “We shall remove whatever rancour is in their breasts, as brothers upon couches facing one another” [15:47]. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N071\">[XIV-N071]<\/a><\/p><p>Thus after spiritual opening (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفتح<\/bdi>) it is said to them: Remember God’s blessing upon you, when you were enemies before being connected to the healer (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الطبيب<\/bdi>); He united your hearts, and by His blessing you became brothers who love one another. You were at the brink of a pit of the fire of severance and veiling (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القطيعة والحجاب<\/bdi>), and He rescued you from it. By such an explanation God makes His signs—that is, His self-disclosures (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تجلياته<\/bdi>)—clear, so that you may be guided to witness His Essence in the lights of His attributes. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"All schools ( المذاهب ) have experienced disagreement and division in fundamentals and branches, save the ways of the Sufis ( مذاهب الصوفية ): these, says Ibn ʿAjība, agree in their beginning and their end, for their beginning is spiritual struggle ( المجاهدة ) and their end is witnessing ( المشاهدة ). The author of al-Mabāḥith alludes to this: The schools of people stand in disagreement; The school of the Folk stands in concord. Even if they disagree over particular ways ( الطرق ) of reaching the aim, they agree concerning the end. This is unlike the people of outward inquiry ( أهل الظاهر ), whom, in Ibn ʿAjība’s characterization, one finds agreeing only on a few questions, for their method is built upon the preponderance of conjecture ( غلبة الظن ), while the method of the Folk ( القوم ) is grounded in realization through tasting and unveiling ( التحقيق ذوقاً وكشفاً ). Their hearts and spirits, likewise, are united: all are formed by compassion, tenderness, affection, harmony and purity, because they have entered Paradise—the Paradise of gnoses ( جنة المعارف )—and adopted the character traits of the inhabitants of Paradise. God says: “We shall remove whatever rancour is in their breasts, as brothers upon couches facing one another” [15:47]. [XIV-N071] Thus after spiritual opening ( الفتح ) it is said to them: Remember God’s blessing upon you, when you were enemies before being connected to the healer ( الطبيب ); He united your hearts, and by His blessing you became brothers who love one another. You were at the brink of a pit of the fire of severance and veiling ( القطيعة والحجاب ), and He rescued you from it. By such an explanation God makes His signs—that is, His self-disclosures ( تجلياته )—clear, so that you may be guided to witness His Essence in the lights of His attributes. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then commanded them to enjoin what is recognized as good and forbid what is reprehensible. Its connection with the preceding verses is that they remained silent when the Jews stirred discord among them, until they almost fought, and not one of them commanded restraint. God therefore warned them against that provocation, urged unity and commanded them to enjoin right and forbid wrong whenever they saw anything of the kind, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then commanded them to enjoin what is recognized as good and forbid what is reprehensible. Its connection with the preceding verses is that they remained silent when the Jews stirred discord among them, until they almost fought, and not one of them commanded restraint. God therefore warned them against that provocation, urged unity and commanded them to enjoin right and forbid wrong whenever they saw anything of the kind, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":5279,"arabic_characters":3595},{"id":"q3-104","q":"3:104","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:104 · Enjoining good, collective obligation and the command of spiritual presence","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 389–391","cairo":"I:389–391","source_page_first":389,"source_page_last":391,"ar":"وَلْتَكُنْ مِنْكُمْ أُمَّةٌ يَدْعُونَ إِلَى الْخَيْرِ وَيَأْمُرُونَ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَيَنْهَوْنَ عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ وَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الْمُفْلِحُونَ (104)\nقلت: (مِنْ) : للتبعيض لأن الأمر بالمعروف والنهي عن المنكر من فُروض الكفاية إذ لا يصلح له كُلُّ أحد، أو للبيان، أي: كونوا أمة تأمرون بالمعروف، كقوله: كُنْتُمْ خَيْرَ أُمَّةٍ أُخْرِجَتْ لِلنَّاسِ تَأْمُرُونَ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ الخ، و (يأمرون بالمعروف وينْهون عن المنكر) عطف على الخبر، من عطف الخاص على العام للإيذان بفضله.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلْتَكُنْ مِنْكُمْ يا أمة محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم أُمَّةٌ أي: طائفةً يَدْعُونَ إِلَى الْخَيْرِ، وهو كل ما فيه صلاحٌ ديني، أو دنيوى إذا كان يؤول إلى الديني، أو صلاح قلبي أو روحاني، وَيَأْمُرُونَ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وهو ما يستحسنه الطبع ويرتضيه الشرع، وَيَنْهَوْنَ عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ وهو كل ما ينكره الطبعُ السليم والشرع المستقيم، فمن فعل ذلك فأولئك هُمُ الْمُفْلِحُونَ المخصوصون بكمال الفلاح.\nروى عنه عليه الصلاة والسلام: أنه سئل مَنْ خير الناس؟ فقال: «آمرُهم بالمعروف، وأنهاهم عن المنكر، وأتقاهم لله، وأوصلهم للرحم» . وقال أيضاً: «مَنْ أمَرَ بالمَعْروفِ ونَهَى عَنِ المُنْكَرِ كان خَلِيفَةَ اللهِ في أرْضِهِ وخَلِيفَةَ رَسُولِه وخَلِيفَةَ كِتَابِه. وقال على رضي الله عنه: (أفضل الجهاد: الأمر بالمعروف والنهي عن المنكر وشنئان الفاسقين- أي بغضهم- فمن أمر بالمعروف شدَّ ظهرَ المؤمن، ومن نهى عن المنكر أرْغَمَ أنف المنافق، ومن شَنَأَ الفاسقين وغَضب لله غَضبَ الله له) . وقال أبو الدرداء: (لتأمُرُن بالمعروف، ولتنهون عن المنكر، أو ليُسلطن الله عليكم سلطاناً ظالماً، لا يُجِلُّ كبيركم، ولا يرحم صغيركم، ويدعو عليه خيارُكم فلا يستجاب لهم، ويَسْتنصِرون فلا يُنصرون، ويستغفرون فلا يغفر لهم) . وقال حذيفة: (يأتي على الناس زمان لأن تكون فيه جيفة حمار، أحب إليهم من مؤمنٍ يأمرهم بالمعروف وينهاهم عن المنكر) .\nوللمتصدِّي له شروط: العلم بالأحكام، ومراتب الاحْتِسَاب وكيفية إقامتها، والتمكن من القيام بها. ولذلك خاطب الحق تعالى الجميع، وطلب فعل بعضهم، إذ لا يصلح للقيام به إلا البعض، كما هو شأن فرض الكفاية، إذ هو واجب على الكل، بحيث لو تركوه لعوقبوا جميعاً، لكنه يسقط بفعل البعض.\nوالأمر بالمعروف يكون واجباً ومندوباً، على حسب ما يأمر به، والنهي عن المنكر واجب كله لأن جميع ما أنكره الشرع حرام. وأما المكروه فليس بمنكر، فيستحب الإرشاد إلى تركه. والأظهر أن العاصي يجب أن ينهى عما يرتكبه هو لأنه يجب عليه تركه، فلا يسقط بترك أحدهما وجوب الآخر. وقد قال عليه الصلاة والسلام:\n«مُرُوا بالمَعْرُوفِ وإنْ لَمْ تَعمَلُوا بِكُلِّهِ، وانْهَوْا عَنِ المُنْكَرِ وإنْ لم تَنْتَهُوا عنه كُلهُ» .\nالإشارة: (ولتكن منكم أمة) أي: طائفة ينهض حالهم ويدلُّ على الله مقالهم، يدعون إلى الخير العظيم، وهو شهود ذات السميع العليم، ويأمرون بالمعروف بالهمة العلية، وينهون عن المنكر بالحال القوية، فكلُّ من رآهم بالصفا ائتمر وانتهى، وكل من صحبهم بالوفاء أخذ حظه من الغنى بالمكيال الأوفى، إن لله رجالاً من نظر إليهم سعد سعادة لا يشقى بعدها أبداً، فهؤلاء يأمرون بالمعروف وينْهون عن المنكر بالحال دون المقال.\nيُحكى أن بعض الشيوخ مرَّ مع أصحابه بقوم يشربون الخمر تحت شجرة، فأراد أصحابه أن يُغيروا عليهم، فقال لهم: إن كنتم رجالاً فَغِيرُوا عليهم بحالكم دون مقالكم، فتوجهوا إلى الله بهممهم، فإذا القوم قد كسروا الأواني، وجاءوا إلى\nالشيخ تائبين. وكذلك قضية معروف الكرخي مع أصحاب السفينة، الذين كانوا مشتغلين باللهو واللعب، فقال له أصحابه: ادع عليهم، فقال: اللهم كما فرَّحتَهم في الدنيا ففرِّحْهُم في الآخرة، فتابوا على يده جميعاً. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم أعاد النهى عن الفرقة، تأكيدا لذمها، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلْتَكُنْ مِنْكُمْ أُمَّةٌ يَدْعُونَ إِلَى الْخَيْرِ وَيَأْمُرُونَ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَيَنْهَوْنَ عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ وَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الْمُفْلِحُونَ (104)","quran_en":"104. Let there be from among you a community who call to what is good, enjoin what is recognized as right, and forbid what is reprehensible. Those are the ones who prosper.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Whether “from among you” denotes a subset or the whole community","html":"<p>I say: “From among you” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مِنْكُمْ<\/bdi>)—the particle min (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مِنْ<\/bdi>)—can be partitive (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">للتبعيض<\/bdi>), because enjoining right and forbidding wrong is among the collective obligations (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فروض الكفاية<\/bdi>), not every person being suited to it. Or min can be explanatory (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">للبيان<\/bdi>): that is, “Be a community that enjoins what is right,” as in “You are the best community brought forth for humankind: you enjoin what is right ...” [3:110]. “They enjoin what is right and forbid what is wrong” is coordinated with the predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخبر<\/bdi>), the mention of the particular after the general (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عطف الخاص على العام<\/bdi>) to show its special merit. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N072\">[XIV-N072]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “From among you” ( مِنْكُمْ )—the particle min ( مِنْ )—can be partitive ( للتبعيض ), because enjoining right and forbidding wrong is among the collective obligations ( فروض الكفاية ), not every person being suited to it. Or min can be explanatory ( للبيان ): that is, “Be a community that enjoins what is right,” as in “You are the best community brought forth for humankind: you enjoin what is right ...” [3:110]. “They enjoin what is right and forbid what is wrong” is coordinated with the predicate ( الخبر ), the mention of the particular after the general ( عطف الخاص على العام ) to show its special merit. [XIV-N072]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Calling to every form of good that leads to religious flourishing","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Let there be from among you,” O community of Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, “a community,” that is, a party, “who call to what is good (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخير<\/bdi>),” meaning whatever brings about religious wellbeing, or worldly wellbeing when it leads to religious wellbeing, or the wellbeing of heart and spirit. “They enjoin what is recognized as right (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المعروف<\/bdi>),” which sound disposition finds beautiful and the revealed Law approves, “and forbid what is reprehensible (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المنكر<\/bdi>),” which sound nature and the upright Law repudiate. Those who do so “are the ones who prosper,” especially distinguished by the perfection of success.<\/p><p>The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is reported to have been asked, “Which people are best?” and to have replied: “Those who most enjoin what is right, most forbid what is wrong, are most mindful of God, and most maintain family ties.” He is also quoted as saying: “Whoever enjoins right and forbids wrong is God’s vicegerent on His earth, the vicegerent of His Messenger, and the vicegerent of His Book.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N073\">[XIV-N073]<\/a><\/p><p>ʿAlī, may God be pleased with him, said: “The best striving (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجهاد<\/bdi>) is to enjoin right, forbid wrong, and detest the transgressors”—that is, hate them. “Whoever enjoins right strengthens the believer’s back; whoever forbids wrong humiliates the hypocrite; and whoever detests the transgressors and grows angry for God, God grows angry on his behalf.” Abū al-Dardāʾ said: “You shall surely enjoin right and forbid wrong, or God will set over you an unjust ruler who will neither honour your elders nor show mercy to your young. Your best people will pray against him without being answered, seek help without receiving it, and seek forgiveness without being forgiven.” Ḥudhayfa said: “A time will come when the corpse of a donkey will be more beloved to people than a believer who enjoins them to right and forbids them from wrong.” <span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: The chains of these cited sayings have not been individually authenticated.]<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N073\">[XIV-N073]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Let there be from among you,” O community of Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, “a community,” that is, a party, “who call to what is good ( الخير ),” meaning whatever brings about religious wellbeing, or worldly wellbeing when it leads to religious wellbeing, or the wellbeing of heart and spirit. “They enjoin what is recognized as right ( المعروف ),” which sound disposition finds beautiful and the revealed Law approves, “and forbid what is reprehensible ( المنكر ),” which sound nature and the upright Law repudiate. Those who do so “are the ones who prosper,” especially distinguished by the perfection of success. The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is reported to have been asked, “Which people are best?” and to have replied: “Those who most enjoin what is right, most forbid what is wrong, are most mindful of God, and most maintain family ties.” He is also quoted as saying: “Whoever enjoins right and forbids wrong is God’s vicegerent on His earth, the vicegerent of His Messenger, and the vicegerent of His Book.” [XIV-N073] ʿAlī, may God be pleased with him, said: “The best striving ( الجهاد ) is to enjoin right, forbid wrong, and detest the transgressors”—that is, hate them. “Whoever enjoins right strengthens the believer’s back; whoever forbids wrong humiliates the hypocrite; and whoever detests the transgressors and grows angry for God, God grows angry on his behalf.” Abū al-Dardāʾ said: “You shall surely enjoin right and forbid wrong, or God will set over you an unjust ruler who will neither honour your elders nor show mercy to your young. Your best people will pray against him without being answered, seek help without receiving it, and seek forgiveness without being forgiven.” Ḥudhayfa said: “A time will come when the corpse of a donkey will be more beloved to people than a believer who enjoins them to right and forbids them from wrong.” [Translator’s note: The chains of these cited sayings have not been individually authenticated.] [XIV-N073]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Capacity, knowledge and the distinction between required and recommended action","html":"<p>The person who undertakes the duty requires qualifications: knowledge of the rulings (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلم بالأحكام<\/bdi>), of the degrees of moral intervention (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مراتب الاحتساب<\/bdi>), of how to carry it out, and the ability to discharge it. That is why the Real addresses all but requests action from some: only a portion are fitted for it. This is the nature of a collective obligation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فرض الكفاية<\/bdi>), which rests on everyone in such a way that, if all neglect it, all are liable, but whose performance by some discharges the rest. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N072\">[XIV-N072]<\/a><\/p><p>Enjoining what is right can be obligatory or recommended, depending on what is being enjoined. Forbidding what is wrong is entirely obligatory, since whatever the Law condemns in this technical sense is forbidden (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حرام<\/bdi>). As for what is merely disliked (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المكروه<\/bdi>), it is not “wrong” in that sense, so encouraging one to leave it is recommended. The stronger view, he says, is that a person who himself commits a sin must nevertheless forbid that same sin to others, because he has two duties—to desist from it and to forbid it—and neglect of one does not remove the obligation of the other. The Prophet is quoted as saying: “Command right even if you do not perform all of it; and forbid wrong even if you do not desist from all of it.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N074\">[XIV-N074]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The person who undertakes the duty requires qualifications: knowledge of the rulings ( العلم بالأحكام ), of the degrees of moral intervention ( مراتب الاحتساب ), of how to carry it out, and the ability to discharge it. That is why the Real addresses all but requests action from some: only a portion are fitted for it. This is the nature of a collective obligation ( فرض الكفاية ), which rests on everyone in such a way that, if all neglect it, all are liable, but whose performance by some discharges the rest. [XIV-N072] Enjoining what is right can be obligatory or recommended, depending on what is being enjoined. Forbidding what is wrong is entirely obligatory, since whatever the Law condemns in this technical sense is forbidden ( حرام ). As for what is merely disliked ( المكروه ), it is not “wrong” in that sense, so encouraging one to leave it is recommended. The stronger view, he says, is that a person who himself commits a sin must nevertheless forbid that same sin to others, because he has two duties—to desist from it and to forbid it—and neglect of one does not remove the obligation of the other. The Prophet is quoted as saying: “Command right even if you do not perform all of it; and forbid wrong even if you do not desist from all of it.” [XIV-N074]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Those whose state itself calls others to God","html":"<p>“Let there be from among you a community”: a company whose spiritual state (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال<\/bdi>) impels others to rise, and whose words point towards God. They summon to the greatest good, the witnessing of the Essence of the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شهود ذات السميع العليم<\/bdi>); they enjoin right through their exalted spiritual aspiration (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الهمة العلية<\/bdi>) and forbid wrong through the strength of their state (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحال القوية<\/bdi>). Whoever sees them in purity obeys and refrains; whoever accompanies them in fidelity obtains his full share of spiritual wealth. God has men at the sight of whom someone attains a happiness after which he shall never be wretched. Such men enjoin right and forbid wrong through their state rather than through speech (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بالحال دون المقال<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>It is related that a shaykh once passed with his companions by some men drinking wine beneath a tree. His companions wished to rush upon them. He said: “If you are men, change their conduct by your state, not by your speech.” They directed their spiritual aspirations toward God and the men immediately broke their vessels and came to the shaykh in repentance.<\/p><p>So too is the incident of Maʿrūf al-Karkhī and the people on a boat who were occupied with amusement and play. His companions told him to pray against them. He said: “O God, just as You have made them happy in this world, make them happy in the Hereafter.” All of them repented through him. Through God is success, and He is the Guide to the even way. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N075\">[XIV-N075]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"“Let there be from among you a community”: a company whose spiritual state ( حال ) impels others to rise, and whose words point towards God. They summon to the greatest good, the witnessing of the Essence of the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing ( شهود ذات السميع العليم ); they enjoin right through their exalted spiritual aspiration ( الهمة العلية ) and forbid wrong through the strength of their state ( الحال القوية ). Whoever sees them in purity obeys and refrains; whoever accompanies them in fidelity obtains his full share of spiritual wealth. God has men at the sight of whom someone attains a happiness after which he shall never be wretched. Such men enjoin right and forbid wrong through their state rather than through speech ( بالحال دون المقال ). It is related that a shaykh once passed with his companions by some men drinking wine beneath a tree. His companions wished to rush upon them. He said: “If you are men, change their conduct by your state, not by your speech.” They directed their spiritual aspirations toward God and the men immediately broke their vessels and came to the shaykh in repentance. So too is the incident of Maʿrūf al-Karkhī and the people on a boat who were occupied with amusement and play. His companions told him to pray against them. He said: “O God, just as You have made them happy in this world, make them happy in the Hereafter.” All of them repented through him. Through God is success, and He is the Guide to the even way. [XIV-N075]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then repeated the prohibition on division, underscoring his censure of it, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then repeated the prohibition on division, underscoring his censure of it, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":5506,"arabic_characters":3249}];
